window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Aghor","href":"articles/aghor.html","kind":"article","text":"Ancient of Space and Time The Creator, The Infinite, The God Soul, God Name Aghor Pronunciation (UH-gorr) Rogue Ancient said to have come from the Timeless Void to create the world. Creator of Time and Space, mortal souls, and the Infinite. Destroyed by the terrible power of the Godform, to whom the God Soul was absorbed, tripartite.","image":"assets/Aghor.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Aszurithice","href":"articles/aszurithice.html","kind":"article","text":"Dragonlord of the Winterlands Dragonlord of the Winterland Name Aszurithice Pronunciation (AZ-ur-ih-THISS) Faction Draconic Kindred “Winter’s dread beauty, borne of majestic rime, where wander ghosts among the frost” — Aszurithice Dragonqueen, The Frostwyrm, Winterdrake, Celurna’s Chosen Dragonlord of the Kindred, born of sin. By her own will, chose to dwell in the lands of winter, to protect her home. It is said the frozen lands are filled with ghosts, the fallen of the old wars against the Hungerer. In the tundra, Celurna’s grace touched the living, and too she chose the daughter of Nathalor as her champion. But now, the hungering tribes grow in number and the ghosts of the fallen are restless. Aszurithice, in draconic form, brings icy death to those who have had their souls stolen.","image":"assets/Aszurithice.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Auralon","href":"articles/auralon.html","kind":"article","text":"Ancient of Air and Sea Ancient of Sky and Sea Name Auralon Pronunciation (AW-rah-lonn) “Without Depth or Form.” — Auralon The Formless Soul, The Formless One Rogue Ancient said to have come from the Timeless Void to create the world. Creator of the winds and waters, and the Infinite. Destroyed by Maegoroth during the calamitous Battle of Dawn, as punishment for the Great Heresy.","image":"assets/Auralon.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Baiji En","href":"articles/baiji.html","kind":"article","text":"Firekeeper of the Thalane Remnants Firekeeper of the Thalane Remnants Name Baiji En","image":"assets/Baiji.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Cadarinost Falls","href":"articles/cadarinost-falls.html","kind":"article","text":"The Slaying of the Phantom Drake The Slaying of the Phantom Drake Name Cadarinost Falls","image":"assets/Cadarinost Falls.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Caurobor","href":"articles/caurobor.html","kind":"article","text":"Ancient of the Light Ancient of the Light Name Caurobor Pronunciation (KAIR-uh-borr) “Free From Shadow.” — Caurobor The Light Soul, The Light Rogue Ancient said to have come from the Timeless Void to create the world. Creator of the first light, fire, the Suns, and the Infinite. Destroyed by Maegoroth during the calamitous Battle of the Dawn, as punishment for the Great Heresy.","image":"assets/Caurobor.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Filibeth","href":"articles/filibeth.html","kind":"article","text":"Princess of the Glaurfeldt Princess of the Glaurfeldt Name Filibeth Faction Draconic Kindred","image":"assets/Filibeth.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Galand Helviath","href":"articles/galand-helviath.html","kind":"article","text":"Vestige of the Light Soul Warrior of the Kindred Name Galand Helviath Pronunciation (GAH-lind HELL-vee-ath) Faction Draconic Kindred “I followed someone who betrayed me before, and I will not let that happen again.” — Galand to Heldranc, upon the creation of their covenant Born: IV-470 Homeland: The Glaurfeldt Race: Starfolk Faction: The Draconic Kindred Soldier of the Glaurfeldt, raised within the Kingdom of Mano in the fourth century of the fourth era. Transported two millennia into the future by manner of arcane intervention. Latent power of the Light burns within his soul. With great strength, Galand fights against the forces of darkness and ruin. The boy born under the stars, raised by the moon, and freed under the suns. Galand was born in the Glaurfeldt under its everlasting, speckled night sky. Around the age of two, his parents traveled on the Stony Road through the K'bas, toward Moor Sallian. At night in camp, while his parents slept, little Galand crawled out from his bedroll toward a noise in the wood. Fearless and curious, he wandered, toward the likely sound of a night critter. By morning, he had not returned. His parents searched tirelessly, bereaved but hopeful, but could not find the boy, Galand, not older than six years old, had gotten lost. He was alone in the forest, scavenging for plants and shelter as he could, surviving with the will of one far beyond his years. Weeks later, he found his way to the edge of the forest and saw the King's Road, a well-traveled highway running east and west through the Glaurfeldt. Here, he wandered on its beaten stones for hours until he crossed paths with a contingent of armored Knights patrolling the road. The soldiers were hesitant at first from tensions of war brewing in the south. To them this child was wandering from the wood and decided it would be best to take him to the safety of the king's walls. By the age of ten, young Galand began training in the arts of combat. In his first year of practice, he displayed a potential far greater than his peers. He was placed with older adolescents - who quickly found themselves defeated by the strength and movement of the boy. One day during a clash with a rival trainee, as he wailed into the metal of his opponent's shield, Galand's weapon glowed with a faint luminosity. The strike nearly crushed the armor of his sparring partner. Questioning the occurrence, Galand spoke with his superiors and the clergy, who told him to continue his path, for it was the blessing of moonlight. And so, into adulthood, Galand trained and honed his strength, not yet bested by any of Mano's lesser knights. In IV-470, at the age of twenty, Galand noticed an orange-cloaked man in the castle training courtyard. This would be the future-sent ranger, Vaerek Rathkin, whose meeting would forever alter the course of the Manoan's life. In two weeks time, by king's decree the knights would once again invade the K'bas forest, to scour the land of the corruption of Filibeth, the forsaken princess. Galand would be commanded to lead Vaerek and the foreigners into the forest, a gamble by the King's clergy that would fail. Upon reaching the Shrine of the Stars, a sanctuary of the forest-folk deep in the forest, Galand came upon the magic of Filibeth and would forsake the blessing of the moon. Soon, it would be learned that Galand was in fact a star-child, one born of the woods - only adopted by the Kingdom of Mano, that Mano's faith was false, and Filbeth and her people were peaceful. With this revelation, Galand fought against his former brethren in confusion and sorrow, attempting to defend the forest-folk against the onslaught. The defense would fail, as Mano himself would descend upon the burning landscape, showcasing a grave alliance with a dragon called Heldranc. In the hour of Mano's victory, the Godtree at the center of the K'bas would be cleaved down, the remaining forest-folk would be slain, and Filibeth, the target of the assault, would be prepared for execution. As the axe-edge of Glaidrogh fell towards Filibeth's nape, an elder wizard appeared through a portal, and by some arcane miracle, froze time. The mage ushered Galand and the foreigners through the rift, while simultaneously casting torrents of deadly sorcery upon the outmatched knights, before Mano himself split him from shoulder to hip. The sacrifice of the wizard allowed the rest to escape, leaving the Glaurfeldt behind. They would come-to on a sparkling, white sand beach. Here, a blue sky with two suns shone above and the sounds of sea-birds filled the air. Stunned, Galand was, the chaos of battle now silenced by the blinding sun and tranquility. In his bewilderment, Galand crashed his hammer down upon a stone, expecting his blessed power to have been taken in his betrayal. But it was not. For Galand still holds the power bestowed to him, and now, seeks to follow the strange foreigners in the Land of God. With them, he seeks to understand his place, his power, and his purpose in a new world.","image":"assets/Galand's First Flight.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Hanto Han","href":"articles/hanto-han.html","kind":"article","text":"Warrior of Kaiden Warrior of Kaiden Name Hanto Han","image":"assets/Hanto Han.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Heldranc","href":"articles/heldranc.html","kind":"article","text":"Dragonlord of Nathalor Lord of Dragons Name Heldranc Pronunciation (Hel-DRAHNK) Faction Draconic Kindred “O mighty lord, arisen of stone and flame, betwixt cold and ash from ancient and grim legend” — Heldranc Father of Dragons, The Dragonlord, Dragon of the Old Blood, King of Nathalor Great Lord of Dragons, ancient and wise, born before the Darkness first swept the land, in the second age. After the extermination of the dragons by the Dragonslayers of Raim, Heldranc fled into the underworld. Within the Starry Kingdom, he lived, allegiant to House Manos, even in its madness, until the House’s betrayal. Founded the kingdom of Nathalor, to save his kin. Within it, the weathered lord bides his time, preparing his Kindred for what is to come.","image":"assets/Heldranc Flies.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Hesk","href":"articles/hesk.html","kind":"article","text":"Ancient of the Wild Ancient of the Wilds Name Hesk Pronunciation (HESK) “The Wild.” — Hesk The Wild Soul, The Green Father Rogue Ancient said to have come from the Timeless Void to create the world. Creator of earth and stone, flora, and the Infinite. Destroyed by Maegoroth during the calamitous Battle of the Dawn, as punishment for the Great Heresy.","image":"assets/Hesk.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Kojin En","href":"articles/kojin.html","kind":"article","text":"Swordsman of the Thalane Remnants Swordsman of the Thalane Remnants Name Kojin En","image":"assets/Kojin.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Kuroishi","href":"articles/kuroishi.html","kind":"article","text":"Kaiden Mountain Village Kaiden Mountain Village Name Kuroishi","image":"assets/Ashforge Kuroishi.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Maegoroth","href":"articles/maegoroth.html","kind":"article","text":"Prime Cosmic Lord Prime Cosmic Lord Name Maegoroth Pronunciation (MAY-gorr-awth) “Doom Bringer.” — Maegoroth The Destroyer, The Elder Soul, The Great One Highest Ancient said to have come from the Timeless Void to destroy the world. Creator of the Cosm, and shaper of planets, stars, and the void, where no time or form exists. Corruptor of the Dark Soul. Destroyed by Aghor during the calamitous Battle of the Dawn, so that the world would be saved.","image":"assets/Maegoroth.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Mano","href":"articles/mano.html","kind":"article","text":"King of the Glaurfeldt Lord of the Glaurfeldt Name Mano Pronunciation (MAH-noh) Faction Demonic Armies “By the bountiful crimson moon shall the corruption be purged. Darkness shall never come to this Kingdom of God.” — Mano King of the Mount, Liege of Blood, The Red Tyrant, Blessed of the Moon Great Lord of the Glaurfeldt, who protects the entirety of the region under the power of Cressana, the Crimson Moon. After the moon rose, casting its fell light across the land, the king and his knights scoured the realm of the corruption of his forsaken daughter, Filibeth. The scriptures spoke of an Elder God from another world, from which Mano’s purpose was discovered.","image":"assets/Mano.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Mintas Fae","href":"articles/mintas-fae.html","kind":"article","text":"Lord of the Everdark Lord of the Everdark Name Mintas Fae Pronunciation (MINN-tahss FAY) Faction Demonic Armies “Gods’ splinter soaks in the river of dreams. Uyin has fallen, Uyin has risen. As shall we. Whilst the Red Moon shall rise, to bless us all.” — Mintas Dark Spirit, Everdark King, Gloomlord Great demonic lord from the underworld. Lord of the Everdark, below the green domain, where the shadows linger like fog. When the Black Lord came from the west during the Gloom, from beyond the inferno, the Dark Spirit arose to listen. Now, the sylvan groves rot and wither, shadows pool in deep places, and the umbral words etch themselves into the roots. The words of Fae are translations from the darkness. Thus, is spoken, after the transformation comes to pass, the night shall come, and by the grace of the moon shall we ascend.","image":"assets/Mintas Fae.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Montmorian","href":"articles/montmorian.html","kind":"article","text":"Dragonlord of the Godland Dragonlord of the Godland Name Montmorian Pronunciation (Mont-MOR-ee-inn) Faction Draconic Kindred “Immortal and pure, of a right exalted and kingly, lord of land divine” — Montmorian King of Fala, Heir-Prince of Nathalor, Dragon Prince, Kingdrake, The Mighty and Immortal Dragon Dragonlord of the Kindred, born of sin. By his father’s command, was sent to dwell in the lands of Golos, to learn the ways of the lords of yore. Was a general of Fala in the twilight years of the fourth era, and was chosen for the throne following the loss of King Gadros at the turn of the age. A divine instrument of the Holy Three, by their protection was he and the people of his city saved.","image":"assets/Montmorian and the Alabaster Blade.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Nyralshirad","href":"articles/nyralshirad.html","kind":"article","text":"Dragonlord of the Great Wilds Dragonlord of the Woodland Name Nyralshirad Pronunciation (Nih-RALL-shih-RAHD) Faction Draconic Kindred “Sylvan rancor of thorn and vine, sunken deep, vengeant and moulding” — Nyralshirad Cursebane Vengeant, Greendrake, Bane of the Huntress, The Sunken King Dragonlord of the Kindred, born of sin. By his father’s command, was sent to dwell in the forest lands of Hesk. Within the gnarled forests of Athag’ir, the sylvan son lived for centuries, lending his brutal power in the endless war against the Feral Huntress and her spawn of curseblood. Known to have taken domain below the roots, in the sunken city of ancient Ajin’tatel. On the brink of being lost to madness and curse, of dark corruption, Nyralshirad was restored by his kin. If Fae, Shiok, and their slaves wish violence, so shall it be delivered them.","image":"assets/Nyralshirad.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Perstrin Naba","href":"articles/perstrin-naba.html","kind":"article","text":"Master Sorcerer Master Sorcerer Name Perstrin Naba","image":"assets/Perstrin Naba.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Pethengorom","href":"articles/pethengorom.html","kind":"article","text":"Dragonlord of the Crystal Land Dragonlord of the Crystal Land Name Pethengorom Pronunciation (PEH-THENG-gor-ahm) Faction Draconic Kindred “Threads watched and woven, across crystal and corpse, infinite in their cascade” — Pethengorom The Weaver, King of Crystal, Drake of the Gem Waste, Gemdrake, Manadrake, The Sagedragon, Spinner of Threads Dragonlord of the Kindred, born of sin. By his father’s command, was sent to dwell in the lands of Crystal, far to the south, to learn the powers of the gem wastes. A wizened sage who studies what he calls the Current, by which he provides illuminating counsel to the Kindred. As souls are threaded, woven through time, the weaver pulls upon them with great sorcery.","image":"assets/Pethengorom.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Phantom Cavern","href":"articles/phantom-cavern.html","kind":"article","text":"Refuge of the Phantoms Refuge of the Phantoms Name Phantom Cavern","image":"assets/Phantom Cavern.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Revenant Host","href":"articles/revenant-host.html","kind":"article","text":"Risen Soldiers of Darkness Risen Soldiers of Darkness Name Revenant Host Faction Demonic Armies","image":"assets/Revenant Host.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Salaseth","href":"articles/salaseth.html","kind":"article","text":"Ancient of War Ancient of War Name Salaseth Pronunciation (SAH-lah-seth) “Great Warrior.” — Salaseth Dragon-God, The Harbinger, The Dragon Soul, Warden of the World Spawn of the Rogue Ancients said to have been created to defend the world against annihilation. Father of the Archdragons. Destroyed by Maegoroth’s design during the calamitous Battle of the Dawn, so that the world would go unguarded.","image":"assets/Salaseth II.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Saoirse","href":"articles/saoirse.html","kind":"article","text":"High Druidess of Arleth High Druidess of Arleth Name Saoirse Pronunciation (SEER-sha) Faction Draconic Kindred , Druids of Heartroot Sanctuary “Mother of groves, weaver of wreaths, and maiden of flowers: let me be rooted in Your peace, woven by Your gentleness, and ever returned to the blooming path of all living things.” — Saoirse's daily prayer to Arleth Heartroot Sanctuary Balamwood Arlethian clergy Bound to Aoife Born to the clergy of Arleth in the Balamwood. Bound in spirit to the hawk Aoife. Ward of Vaerek Rathkin. Saoirse’s entrance to the world came with the first flowers of the Balamwood’s spring, or so she had always been told. It was a detail the ovates of Heartroot Sanctuary interpreted as an omen, as if the season of one's birth could foretell how their fate would bloom. She was born to prominent druids within a veiled Arlethian order and raised with their devotion to bestial magic and the healing arts. From her earliest years, nature's gifts revealed themselves to her with accelerated clarity. Lineage and talent together made her path to her parents' place in the priesthood an unquestioned inheritance to all around her. She embraced it, for a time, but stillness in one course did not always come easy to her. As a child, her attention strayed from the forest’s heart to its edges, drawn to the thinning of trees and beyond. The certainty of her path would finally shift with the arrival of Vaerek Rathkin. An outsider with a shrouded past, Vaerek was the first stranger in her short life to grace their halls. The young ranger’s presence unsettled the community despite their Archdruid's invitation, but that did not stop Saoirse's parents from opening their home to him as he came to embrace their ways. He carried himself with lessons learned outside the grove and instincts that leaned too readily toward tooth and steel; though perhaps these features should have repelled her, they instead awakened a world of interest in her, and she sought him out with relentless fascination. What began as a child’s curiosity settled over time into a more enduring bond. As he carved out his place among her people, no longer merely a tolerated outsider but a protector in his own right, he became a kinship nearer to family than duty. When he was named her Wreath Warden, she could barely contain her joy. Fostering her growing hunger for exploration, Vaerek and her parents brought her on expeditions to villages and less-protected wilds. There she witnessed the work of the other druids and their wardens, learning early that the world’s wounds would not remain distant. It was during one such journey that she found an abandoned hatchling in a destroyed nest. She carried the young bird home and gave her the name Aoife. Through a ceremonial rite, their spirits were bound as sisters, awareness intertwined as Aoife grew by her side into a mighty hawk. Years passed as Saoirse cultivated her calling. The lessons from her warden took deeper root with each passing season. Every scouting mission with Vaerek quietly shaped her worldview, and each encounter pressed a simple truth deeper into her: hesitation is its own kind of harm. So when she alone saw signs of Shiokian infiltration appear near their border, she did not seek counsel as instructed. She acted. What followed still resists clear memory, coming to her only in fragments: claws tearing into flesh and sight alike, the disorienting collapse of control, and her attempt at justice twisted into a brutal example for sacrifice. Her survival came as an intervention beyond her own strength, owed to her Archdruid's resurfacing of a long-buried power. Her soul was rescued from the Hunt, but her salvation came at a cost that lingered long after their wounds closed, a fracture not easily named. As her role in Heartroot's circle grew closer, that fracture only deepened. Where she once felt her Goddess in every breath of wind and turn of leaves, only stillness remained. Prayer became a discipline rather than a comfort. She dared not speak of it aloud despite Vaerek’s concern or the way his protective watch hovered over her every step. An unspoken tension settled between them, and she could not bear to remain where it lingered. Whispers of unrest from the walls of Fala began to carry rumors of Shiokian corruption. Given another chance to confront her past failures, she invoked a sanctioned departure through the Rite of Pilgrimage, a path out that would foster pride rather than further scrutiny from her peers. With Aoife, she left her family behind to follow the signs southward until she encountered a band of travelers. After helping them fend off thieves, she offered her healing to them in exchange for an escort to the capital. Somewhere farther than the reach of the Balamwood, amid creeping curses and uncertain roads, her journey would test what remained of her faith and determine what it might yet become. Either way, she would not return unchanged.","image":"assets/Saoirse.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Silar Scorria","href":"articles/silar-scorria.html","kind":"article","text":"Dragon Scholar Dragon Scholar Name Silar Scorria","image":"assets/Silar Scorria.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Sthistu","href":"articles/sthistu.html","kind":"article","text":"Ancient of the Dark Ancient of the Dark Name Sthistu Pronunciation (Sih-THISS-too) “Dark One.” — Sthistu The Shadow, The Dark Soul Rogue Ancient said to have come from the Timeless Void to create the world. Creator of the dark, the night, the first shadow, and the Infinite. Destroyed by Salaseth in the Celestial Sands, so the corruption in the Dark Soul would be purified.","image":"assets/Sthistu.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Vaerek Rathkin","href":"articles/vaerek.html","kind":"article","text":"Wreathbound Ranger Wreathbound Ranger Name Vaerek Rathkin Pronunciation (VAIR-ek RATH-kin) Faction Draconic Kindred “Vigar'am primori a sek. A sanguin, scal, a scal, mi'aghora. Lygos Amora, lygos Sareen, lygos Horlg, lygos Vana, lygos Mano. Sek Salaseth, te mi'aghora, vigar'am.” — Vaerek's battle incantation Born V-9 Morathi Wreathbound Ranger Heartroot Sanctuary Bastard son of a Morathi noble who chose exile over Tarn. Deserter of Fala. Ranger of Heartroot. Ward of Saoirse. The bastard son a Morathi noble, who chose self-exile over a life of luxurious derision. Vaerek began that life by venturing on trips in the wilderness out of his home city of Tarn. The choice to serve in the military came during this time, where soon-after he accepted a placement far away, across the sea, in the Empire of Fala. A step towards true solitude. Though, in this assignment he didn't last, and deserted in that foreign land, longing for a life outside the rigidity of Morathi heirarchy. He chose to travel the Balamwood following his desertion, by chance, as it seemed a proper place to leave the noise of civilization. Several years were spent living the life of a woodland hermit and working as a forest-guide to the farmers who lived along its fringes. As time went on, the druids within the Balamwood warmed to his presence, allowing him entry into the grove of the Heartroot Circle and an invitation to join its sanctuary. Vaerek found grove society more palatable than anywhere he had been before, even though he butted heads with many a druid. In the eyes of Arleth's clergy, Vaerek was too liberal in his applications of violence. He was never extended the offer to join the clergy, even though he showed an aptitude for Beast magic (precisely, reptilian wildshaping). Archdruid Ulric, seeing another lost beast, did not let discussion of Vaerek from the druids ever leave the realm of simple critique. So, Vaerek continued on at the Grove, finding a place to be, a place where he - although questioned - always had a chance to disprove the worry of druidic criticism. After some time, Vaerek befriended a young druidess by the name of Saoirse. The druidess sought the world beyond the grove, and came to him as one of the few who had such a yearning. Vaerek would go on to teach her the arts of combat and indulge in her wanderlust. He allowed her to often accompany him during his patrols of the Balamwood, coming to view her as his adoptive younger sister. When Saoirse confronted Shiokan followers by herself, Vaerek was the one to find her but not the one to save her. Ulric stepped in, commanding Vaerek to return to the grove and for the girl's family to be gathered. Reluctantly he complied. After witnessing Saoirse grow into adulthood, it was difficult watching her leave for her own journey away from the grove. He missed her deeply for a time, but soon, Ulric would appear at his den's door with a quest. The Archdruid gave Vaerek a mandate to track Saoirse's whereabouts and protect her, as she once again had confronted Shiokans. Setting off without debate, Vaerek followed her path from the Balamwood, southward to walls of the capital of the mighty and immortal Fala. With the druidess so nearby, all would change, as he would be swept up in the Fading. Confused, he awoke in the dunes of the Samafi, thinking his quest ended and his little sister likely dead. Vaerek spent weeks in the desert surviving alone, mourning in self castigation in the heat and the cold of the sands. He was ready to return to Balamwood and relay his failure to the Archdruid. Eventually, his wandering found him at the gates of the township of Mora Mora. In Mora Mora, his ears caught rumor of Falan survivors - so, with kindled, albeit tenuous hope, Vaerek chose to stay. He prayed Saoirse would come through the gates of the town. In his waiting, he volunteered to patrol the desert, gathering resources and slaying sandworms, praying to find her in the sands. Soon, his prayers were answered. Returning from patrol he heard that a new group of 'Foundlings', as the Morans called them, had arrived - with one matching Saoirse's description. Vaerek rushed to find them, only to hear that they had been sent on a quest to earn the trust of the prince. Vaerek bided his time, with hope restored. When word of the group's return came, he rushed to find them. It was her, in the company of others also forsaken to the southern sands from Fala. In his happiness he realized his quest was complete. So he would join them in their wandering journey on a new quest, a story yet to be written. Vaerek's story now is the same as Saoirse's, a knot in the roots of time, the clearing at the end of His path of pink leaves not yet in sight. Fire, and the obsidian of stone, can Vaerek see now among those leaves. May His godtrees watch over them.","image":"assets/Vaerek, At Ease.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Vehem Adahr","href":"articles/vehem-adahr.html","kind":"article","text":"Lord of the Four Pillars Lord of the Four Pillars Name Vehem Adahr Pronunciation (veh-HEHM uh-DARR) Faction Demonic Armies “As prophesied, the Black Storm has come to pass, and we drink upon the power of our prize. Rejoice, for beyond the Auracine, an old king has listened, too.” — Vehem The Black Storm, The Dreaming King, The Deep Dreamer, The Black Dreamer, Nightmare Consort Great demonic lord from the underworld. Said to have risen forth in the wake of the Grand Nightmare of Nereb. His fell reign began when he conquered the Deep Demesne of Uyin, joining the northern, southern, western and eastern kingdoms into a unified sovereignty known as the Four Pillars of Uyin. As the ashes presaged, after the Black Storm, a splinter of God bathes in the river of dreams.","image":"assets/Vehem Adahr.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Vorn","href":"articles/vorn.html","kind":"article","text":"Lord of Enthar Lord of the Darklands of Enthar Name Vorn Pronunciation (VORN) Faction Demonic Armies “One must prostrate utter devotion if thou wishest to hear the unspoken utterances of the Dark. If thy devotion is not absolute, thou shalt hear naught but the gnashing of teeth.” — Vorn The Dark Prince, Lord of Shadow, Prince of Darkness, The Black When the shadow crossed the lands, the royals of Enthar recoiled and hid in their fortresses of stone. The old Darkness came, and through it, Nul ascended to divinity. In his umbral path, the young prince Vorn followed, unafraid. The Dark Prince of Enthar proclaims to speak with what he calls the Living Dark, for it is said muttering voices can be heard from within. Now, petitioners from across the enshadowed country and beyond journey as pilgrims to tell the Lord the secrets they hear, wishing to join his growing congregation. Though he seems as a man, nothing remains of his mortal shell but an aphotic likeness.","image":"assets/Vorn.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Yuo Nanaga","href":"articles/yuo-nanaga.html","kind":"article","text":"Archer of the Thalane Remnants Archer of the Thalane Remnants Name Yuo Nanaga","image":"assets/Yuo Nanaga.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Bel Harath","href":"articles/bel-harath.html","kind":"article","text":"Lord of the Molnaran Inferno","image":"assets/Bel Harath.png","private":true,"owner":"jack"},{"title":"Curse of Olesh","href":"articles/curse-of-olesh.html","kind":"article","text":"Curse of Olesh","image":"assets/Curse of Olesh.png","private":true,"owner":"devin"},{"title":"Cursed of Olesh","href":"articles/cursed-of-olesh.html","kind":"article","text":"Cursed of Olesh","image":"assets/Cursed of Olesh.png","private":true,"owner":"devin"},{"title":"Darmstadt","href":"articles/darmstadt.html","kind":"article","text":"Shield of the Lords","image":"assets/Darmstadt.png","private":true,"owner":"devin"},{"title":"Elraim","href":"articles/elraim.html","kind":"article","text":"The Godslayer","image":"assets/Elraim.jpg","private":true,"owner":"devin"},{"title":"Haethlin in the Dream","href":"articles/haethlin-dream.html","kind":"article","text":"Seer of the Faithful","image":"assets/Haethlin in the Dream .png","private":true,"owner":"jack"},{"title":"Haethlin","href":"articles/haethlin.html","kind":"article","text":"The White, The Silent King, Demon of the Light, King Diviner (HAYTH-linn) King of the Silent City In the east, beyond the Glaurfeldt and across the Sunken Fields, dwells the Silent City and its ponderous ruler, Haethlin the White. A city of sages and prognosticators that ever portent the coming days, gazing into their enchanted reflective pools. In exchange for their prophetic foresight, the Silent City remains unmolested by the ills of the Spawn Wars. Haethlin himself is said to be an old demonic lord, a vigorous soul from before the enslavement of the Deepfolk. Though greatly powerful in his divination, Haethlin seeks ever more to illuminate the shadow — to bring silent certainty to the cold, unknown darkness. The lord is said to have gained his power from a being of pure light, when it long ago sought apostles to aid its pursuit of the Living Dark.","image":"assets/Haethlin.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Imani Valash","href":"articles/imani-valash.html","kind":"article","text":"Cosmic Sorceress","image":"assets/Imani Valash.png","private":true,"owner":"devin"},{"title":"Ito Gangara","href":"articles/ito-gangara.html","kind":"article","text":"Queen of the Core","image":"assets/Ito Gangara.png","private":true,"owner":"jack"},{"title":"Rathalon","href":"articles/rathalon.html","kind":"article","text":"Lord of the Elite","image":"assets/Rathalon.png","private":true,"owner":"devin"},{"title":"Sen Teloch Ini","href":"articles/sen-teloch-ini.html","kind":"article","text":"Dimensional Sorceress","image":"assets/Sen Teloch Ini.png","private":true,"owner":"devin"},{"title":"Telorin","href":"articles/telorin.html","kind":"article","text":"Dragon Scholar","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Alaric","href":"articles/alaric.html","kind":"article","text":"Alaric","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Vallorca","href":"articles/vallorca.html","kind":"article","text":"Vestige of the Formless Soul","image":"assets/Vallorca.png","private":true,"owner":"jack"},{"title":"Yena","href":"articles/yena.html","kind":"article","text":"Vestige of the Dragon Soul","image":"assets/Yena.png","private":true,"owner":"jack"},{"title":"Act III, Chapter XLI","href":"journal.html#iii-xli","kind":"journal","text":"Five remained of Thalane’s order, and that truth sat with them at the fire while the others watched from the rocks, unable to understand a word. Silar, who had followed rumor of dragons across the sea, asked how long they had sought Thalancord. Only the hours Kojin had already seen, Vaerek told him, though the search for the Drake’s brothers and sister had gone on far longer. He named himself Rathkin, a son of a Shasirian mother. Silar took his hand. He was of Shasir as well. Neither had met another Sethian in a long time. He spoke aside to Kojin, who laughed, and called it fate: they had not meant to be at Redroot at all. The Interval had begun, and Thalancord was missing. Silar Scorria, a sethian dragon scholar, speaks at the campfire. The five around the fire were all that was left. Their mentor and most of their companions had been taken by the curse. Vaerek named his companions. Silar noted the blindfold and was told there were other senses than sight. Silar had come here when he learned that a power was fighting Olesh’s venom and that the power might be draconic. He had met Thalancord, as the order all had. The charge given down from Thalane was to find a cure if one existed, and if not to cut the afflicted down and give the bodies to flame. They had come to Redroot on a thin hope, found the village already fallen, and done what the duty required. Kojin En, Kanta Masan Swordsman An interval, Silar said, was the turning of the cycle. Olesh had been slain three times and had risen three times. The old way was to gather militias without letting the curse pool, find where the madness gathered, and threaten it until Olesh came to restore it, and then to kill him. After great storms the land itself announced his return — thunders, rains, winds. Animals went frantic and rabid, forest creatures and household pets turned. Telorin said each country seemed to wear a different face of the same threat, and that something godlike sat behind it. He named Mintas Fae of Hesk, Vehem Adahr of the Samafi, and Vorn of the lands below. Silar poured wine called Tears of the Rains and said that here, at least, the enemy had a name and a season. After the last battles Thalancord had simply vanished. No body. The sky darkened for weeks; when the rains broke and the woods went loud, Olesh was returned. Yuo Nanaga, Ai Ishiin Archer In the morning they would walk a country they had not seen in two years, asking for Thalancord at every door: Kuroishi and its smith at its Ash-Forge, Fort Red Eve, a mountain shrine, Minashi, a Tea Village, and last Kireji in the valley. They would travel together. Telorin and Perstrin set to work on scrolls, for translation. Vaerek asked what Silar knew of the Vigors. Silar called fire into his palm and said he served the first wardens of the world. Vaerek said he used them too, though not as a sorcerer, and walked him from the fire to tell him how that had come to be. Morning came clear. Hanto fetched an odachi and met Galand in the clearing. The blade took on a green light. Galand’s eyes went gold, and they struck, steel against steel. Until Galand pulled back, and he knew what occurred — his soul pulled thin, just as Eig’s glaive had pulled. Hanto called his victory chance. The blade was Kuranata, Blade of the Damura, bound to cut spirit as well as flesh, for blood alone did not always finish Olesh’s work. Silar did not think the enchantment came from Raim. Thalane himself had come from the west, and that was all he knew. Hanto Han, Warrior of Kaiden Vaerek and Saoirse closed the wounds. Then they spoke the news they had spared the night before. A slayer of Raim walked. His name was Eig. He had killed a brother of Thalancord and maimed another, and two more lords had been lost to cataclysm besides. The remnant heard it as a cold thing. Silar had already heard of disaster in his homeland, and of a dragonlord taken there. Whether that force served Olesh, no one could say. The scrolls opened speech between the companie","image":"assets/Silar Scorria.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Act III, Chapter XL","href":"journal.html#iii-xl","kind":"journal","text":"The common chamber was quiet when Galand and Vaerek entered, exhausted. Filibeth was there, awaiting Galand’s return. Pethengorom recovered in his chamber. Saoirse was asleep and awoke to their entering the central pool. Cadarinost’s death sat foremost in Vaerek’s mind; Heldranc’s anguish and Montmorian’s grief still moved in him. The next day, Vaerek went to Montmorian in his sanctum, who was in silent grief. He spoke his condolence for the twin. They had hatched together and never been far apart, and Montmorian had watched him die with nothing in his power to stop it. Wars between kingdoms at least declared themselves. This one did not. They named Eig and named the dark and still did not know what either wanted. This ignorance had already taken Rallice in the Winterlands and now nearly two more in the sands. They could not carry that same blindness into the Farland. Vaerek asked that the dragonlords remain behind. If a slayer hunted their kind, the last of them should not walk the road. Montmorian accepted the warning, agreed that they could not delay, and asked to be left. Montmorian, in grief, sits alone in his chamber. Telorin tended his workshop in the days that followed. Rallice’s absence made the work quieter than it should have been. Over the next couple of days, he took Galand’s maul and bound into it stronger runes. Saoirse came while he worked and asked what the leaf had been like in his hands. He explained that he had begun to lose himself in it before Filibeth reached him. She also asked whether they could set down on paper what she had done to cleanse herself of curses, against the chance that the Scourge had taken Darcdressil. On the third day they gathered in the throne room. Heldranc would not watch fear take another of his children. Two roads remained: the Depths, for Darcdressil, or Far Nybei, for Thalancord. Pethengorom stood hooded at the side, well enough now to speak, one forearm gone. He spoke that the Current over Nybei was wrong. Souls there burned too brightly — not dim as the Host, not bright as Filibeth or Galand, only disturbed. He would set them down near the one that still looked as a soul should. They might find Thalancord as they had found Cadarinost: by following his enemy. Vaerek said again that the lords should not travel. Heldranc agreed and forbade the Lords from leaving the sanctuary. Pethengorom would remain and learn how Eig moved. Heldranc wished for he and Aszurithice to bring Cadarinost home from the sand and lay him in the tomb beneath Nathalor — but for now, this was too dangerous. Saoirse asked a place in the city also for the phantoms who had served Cadarinost; it was granted. Perstrin stated he could now recall them back to the sanctuary, but he did not possess the power to read the Current as Pethengorom. Saoirse called Aoife. The green gate opened. They stepped through into autumn. They came out at the edge of a village under trees that twisted as they rose. Woodsmoke hung in the air. A man stood over the dead and watched them arrive. Vaerek smelled blood and drew Valeburn. No one among them spoke the man’s tongue. Telorin used a scroll of tongues, and understood him alone. Kojin, after his duty in Redroot Village. He named himself Kojin En, a swordsman of the Remnants of Thalane. Redroot Village had fallen to the curse, and he and his group had cleared it. The dead were fresh but unnatural — burned, lidless, orange eyes, rotting while still warm. No magic remained on them. Telorin mentioned Thalancord. Kojin struck his chest at Thalancord’s name and said he sought him also. These, he told them, were victims of Olesh. They attempted speech with difficulty. He offered his camp. They walked an hour into the countryside of somewhere in Kaiden, through mist and turning leaves. He named the others as they came into view: Baiji En, his wife; Hanto Han, a warrior of Kaiden; Silar Scorria, a dragon scholar; and Yuo Nanaga, an archer. Speech failed them until Vaerek tried Draconic. S","image":"assets/Montmorian in his Chamber .png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Act III, Chapter XXX","href":"journal.html#iii-xxx","kind":"journal","text":"The green portal left nothing behind. Perstrin’s tattoos burned as he stared at the empty sand where the dragonslayer had stood, and he told Telorin the magic had been pure, cleaner than anything Pethengorom had ever woven, gone in five seconds, with no arcane trace. Montmorian went to his twin on all fours. He nudged Cadarinost’s snout the way a beast tries to wake one who won’t, and a whimper rose from him, the largest of the Dragons. Then he screamed. The sound dropped Vaerek to his knees with blood at his ears. Wings kicked white sand into the twin suns, when Montmorian circled once and flew. Vaerek climbed the corpse. There was no slayer’s blood to take a scent from, only the glaive’s work: deep stabs at the nape, cuts too clean for mundane steel, and a faint char as if the scales had been softened before the killing stroke. Saoirse found no lingering magic on the wounds. Telorin and Perstrin focused their attention on the magic used. With his mimicry, Perstrin opened an oval of his own, Nathalor visible on the far side, so Telorin could watch the working while it lasted. The style matched the future-Perstrin they had met in the Glaurfeldt’s past, though whether that meant the slayer came from then, or now, they could not say. Saoirse laid peaceful rest between Cadarinost’s scales, and white light settled into the wounds. Heldranc found his vessel. Vaerek’s eyes went red-orange, and his gait and voice were no longer his. The dragon-lord clutched Cadarinost’s head and spoke unintelligible Draconic, then demanded the slayer’s description. The witnesses spoke their account: dark armor, a black cloak, a pointed helm, and a glaive sparking green. Terror took the borrowed face. They were all dead, he said — thousands of years dead. The Dragonslayers of Raim. A scream in the distance broke the sorrow. Vaerek, possessed, snatched Valeburn and ran. Saoirse followed her warden’s body; the rest of the group came after, through cream-white dunes, until they found the second body. Pethengorom still breathed. Blood poured from his mouth onto the Celestial Sands and one claw was gone. Montmorian stood over him with a wing lowered. The Crystaldrake, left for dead in the Celestial Sands. Saoirse emptied her staff of its restorative power. Perstrin copied what he could. Telorin took the leaf into his shaking hands; the raw infinite power nearly unmade him before Filibeth answered and asked whether she should bring them back. They gathered and Montmorian became a man again, tears of rage in his eyes. Warmth took them to the Athag’ir Godtree. Filibeth’s pink-white flood closed sinew and crystal while Vaerek’s scaly triage invigorated the wounded dragon. Pethengorom resumed human shape, right hand missing, blood at the mouth, eyes open. The Weaver spoke. A knight, he said. Magically silent. Pain in the back, then again. He had swatted, felt a blast of power, seen blood fall from the sky, and hardened his scales to gemstone. The dragonslayer had left him to bleed out. Cadarinost was a corpse. The word hit him like a second wound. Perstrin and Telorin anchored a portal to the sanctuary and they carried the Weaver through. Heldranc waited at the foot of the throne — the ancient face showed fear. Fifteen stewards laid Pethengorom on cushions. Aszurithice cried that he lived. Nyralshirad stood silent. Heldranc asked two questions: whether the cloak had been black, and whether the helm had a spike. The group confirmed these truths once more. It was a nightmare returned, he said. They were describing the armor of one man in particular. The ebon raiment of the Lord High Dragonslayer of the Empire of Raim, the commander by whose order dragonkind was exterminated. His true name was lost. Among dragons he was known only as Eig, the draconic word for fear. Galand then spoke of the pit in the caverns. In the dark he had seen Vorn on a green plain, channeling the night that sent forth the Revenants, and he had answered with the light within him — an infini","image":"assets/Pethengorom's Fate.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Act III, Chapter XXIX","href":"journal.html#iii-xxix","kind":"journal","text":"The fight ran both sides of the dark. Magical night poured from a pit that dropped without bottom. Revenants climbed it like a tide. Montmorian’s Alabaster Blade threw white light as he cut them down and kicked them back into the drop. Vaerek flew the ledge, Valeburn burning as it struck, and called warning as more souls rose from below. He dropped to a wounded Phantom and dragged her back from the edge. Montmorian wields the Alabaster Blade. Galand’s eyes went pale gold. Wings of pure light unfurled from his back — Telorin’s work made flesh — and the cavern flashed. He dove through the wall of dark, hammer first, then lightning-swapped to Rallice’s greatsword. He rose again trailing light like a fading star. Telorin flanked and put his rapier through one revenant; electricity leapt to the next and both fell. Then he cast Sanguine Mist into the press. The undead drank the void and stood taller. He dropped the spell the moment he understood. Saoirse lashed vitality through the dark, then blessed all allies. Six bodies moved with sudden surety — Arleth’s peace in the step. She called them around the pit to the far fight, where Phantoms were already dying. Galand flew into the void alone. What he saw there he kept. He came back out, hammer sparking, and the last of the near host broke. Darkness receded. Torchlight returned. The pit was empty of souls, as if the night had never been. Galand, alight with the power of the Light Soul. Montmorian stood among the fallen in Fala plate and stared until his eyes glazed. He billowed his cloak. Vaerek healed who he could. To the surviving Phantoms he said they had fought well, and to burn the bodies with respect — these had been people taken. Montmorian did not look back. They walked a cavern of massacre. Void had been here, killed everyone, and left no trace. The tunnel climbed toward heat and morning. They stepped out onto the Celestial Sands. White alabaster dunes. Twin suns. No rain. No thunder. A dragon cried across the sky. It was not Pethengorom. Sand-colored, desert-scaled, it came screeching. Montmorian’s mouth found a smirk — then Vaerek saw the truth. The dragon’s soul was dim, dimmer even than the host. Paired with it rode a soul as bright as a lord’s. The flight failed. Wings folded. The beast tumbled and struck the dunes a thousand feet out. Sand slammed the party. When the dust settled, Cadarinost lay dying. An armored man stood on the corpse, cape billowing, glaive crackling green. The magic pulled from the wound. Vaerek watched the dragon’s soul climb into the slayer. Cadarinost’s head fell. Cadarinost falls to an unknown dragonslayer. Montmorian whispered Brother. Then he screamed it. Vaerek felt the loss in the old bond with dragonkind; steam rose from his eyes instead of tears. Montmorian ran. Light and fire burst as he took his true form and landed on the dunes, throat filling with flame. A green oval opened behind the slayer. He stepped back through it. The portal closed. He was gone. Montmorian, in true form.","image":"assets/Montmorian and the Alabaster Blade.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Act III, Chapter XXVIII","href":"journal.html#iii-xxviii","kind":"journal","text":"Vaerek climbed the hewn stairs with four Phantoms and peered through the rubble at the cave mouth. Rain still poured. Fire still burned. Pethengorom’s flame would not be quelled — blue at the core, crystalline and sorcerous. A few dim souls lingered among the dead. The dragon himself was gone. Vaerek told the Phantoms the truth: the mage who had saved them could take a dragon’s form. Their fear broke into awe, then hope. He called Pethengorom’s name into the storm, but was not answered. Below, the party chose to leave the Phantoms in their sanctuary and press north. Taleera told them more of the host: empty eyes, black hoods, a vigor gone. For years they had come in small, skulking bands. Only in the past year had they marched in numbers, organized, under a storm that had raged ceaselessly. The Caverns of the Phantoms stretch beneath the border of Akath and the Celestial Sands. The caverns ran deep, lined with torches. The sheer pathway dropped into blackness. They walked for a time until two dwellers stopped Montmorian and pointed at his chest. The sigil on his Fala armor was the sigil of their enemy, the Revenants. Telorin spoke the thought no one wanted: perhaps the Revenant Host were Falans taken in the Fading, corrupted by something darker. Montmorian’s stare went distant. Saoirse turned and trudged back to the entrance to see with her own eyes. Outside, the canyon was a sea of burning dead. Dragon-fire did not touch her. She turned bodies until she found what she feared: blackened plate that held the symbol of the Holy Three, cloaks dyed or charred over a blue that Rallice had worn. She cut a breastplate free and tore a scrap of the old color. In the thunder she paused and asked Arleth if that sea of death was what the pilgrimage had meant. Ulric’s warning returned to her: the path she walked would lead to ruin. She fled the moans of the dying back into the cave. Saoirse finds that the Revenant Host were Falan soldiers. She put the proof in Montmorian’s hands. He stared a long time. Then he swore that whoever had done this would feel their wrath, and with a shout he hurled plate and cloak into the dark below. They walked on along a ledge above a drop that had no bottom. Vaerek heard movement far down — too large for vermin — and then a pulse that was not natural. Galand heard it next. Darkness poured through the cavern like a tide, swallowing torchlight. Magic filled the air, high and dark, with no single source. Souls rose from the pit and reached the edge. Montmorian drew the Sword of Torados, an ancient alabaster blade of a Val'norain that threw its own light. Vaerek’s cloak split into wings. Telorin laid haste on Galand. The host stepped out of the dark as if walking on open air. Fallen Falan knights comprise much of the Revenant Host. Montmorian did not hold back. The glowing sword cut and kicked the first revenant back into the dark. Galand’s hammer answered with thunder. Vaerek struck from above. Saoirse, for the first time, went on the offensive: an icicle grown with Aszurithice’s precision, vitality sparking as it lodged in a throat. They were undead. The fight spilled along the ledge. Revenants swung with empty, inhuman strength; void-magic slid off Galand, ineffective. Phantoms fought at their side. One of their guides fell. Saoirse put a glowing hand on the survivor’s shoulder and told him to strike for his friend. Telorin, back to the dark, heard whispers from within. He answered with a cloud of rust. Vaerek healed, called Heldranc’s name, and rose again. An orb of light bloomed deeper in the cavern. What more waited in the dark was unknown. Private No private chapters are filed yet.","image":"assets/Phantom Cavern.png","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Calendar: What does the calendar look like?","href":"codex/calendar.html#what-does-the-calendar-look-like","kind":"codex","text":"The Valoran Calendar was created and is maintained by the Great Scholars of Valoris. As are all accounts of history and lore that are documented in this encyclopedia. Cultures inside their own respective locales might use different calendars or their own structures of time and history, but the Valoran Calendar is widely recognized as the authority [for simplicity of gameplay]. The Calendar year is split into four seasons, with two months in each season. Months are split into 50 days - 5 weeks of 10 days. A special week, Godfall - to commemerate what was lost - heralds the onset of autumn and lasts for 7 days. Thus, one year is 407 days. 1 year = 8 months, 1 month = 5 weeks, 1 week = 10 days, 1 day = 25 hours","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Calendar: The days:","href":"codex/calendar.html#the-days","kind":"codex","text":"The days of the week in the Valoran Calendar are as follows. There are three names. The Aghorian name is often used, as it is quick to say. The common name is not often used in full, but is often used shortened, as in \"I plan to visit the chapel on Rest.\", or further shortened as in \"The tavern was very loud on 'Mem'.\" (Remembrance) - cultural phrasing is ever changing. The literal translation is less commonly used, which is simply a direct translation of the Aghorian name: For Vidras, 'Vi' is life, and 'Dras' is day, therefore, it is 'Life Day'. (Aghorian) Name (Common) Name Literal Translation Vidras Day of Revival Life Day Perdras Day of Preparation Prepare Day Caradras Day of Work Create Day Wisdras Day of Longing Wish Day Sedras Day of Conflict War Day Toidras Day of Struggle Toil Day Mirdras Day of Laments Mourn Day Mandras Day of Remembrance Memory Day Gordras Day of Fortitude Strong Day Redras Day of Rest Rest Day An artists depiction of a Valoran librarium.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Calendar: The months:","href":"codex/calendar.html#the-months","kind":"codex","text":"In the Valoran Calendar, there are two months per season, with a special, seven-day transition week from summer to autumn known as Godfall. The months were so named by predecessor sages of the Valoran Archives. Some of their names formalize and entitle certain historical events that occurred throughout recorded time within their fifty day length. The Valoran Calendar was revised toward the end of the Fourth Era, and may be revised whenever the Great Scholars deem appropriate - though rare, names of the months are ever evolving. Winter 1. Wintermorn, so named for Celurna's rime to freeze the ghosts, and the coldest month in Valore. 2. Darkrise, so named for the first city to fall to the Dark in the Second Era. Spring 3. Dawnbreak, so named for the Battle of Dawn, to herald the Third Era. 4. Starsong, so named for the first starfall to begin the Starsong Years, in the Third Era. Summer 5. Secondsun, so named for the two suns, Sura and Sana. 6. Twinmoon, so named for the twin moons, Uma and Ula. Godfall Godfall, so named for the slaying of God by the Lords of Raim. Autumn 7. Highcrown, so named for the defeat of the Eren dominion by Krael, in the Fourth Era. 8. Flamerest, so named for the defeat of Izil in the War of Fire, and for the onset of winter.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Calendar: What year is it?","href":"codex/calendar.html#what-year-is-it","kind":"codex","text":"Elorae is old. Commonly, the millennia have been divided into eras, each rich with history. The beginnings of new eras are due to calamitous, world-changing events that restructure how life is lived. There are six eras. They are labeled 0 through V. You can write them as E-0 - E-V. Eras are changed due to massive global events that affect the world on a fundamental scale. Greater details are in their own section labeled Eras. The current year is V-44. This is the common way years are written and cataloged - a numeral for the era, followed by a dash and the number of year. Therefore, V-44 is the forty-fourth year of the fifth era. Always, the years are named from the Valoran naming convention. They are named at the end of the year, to name the next. For example, V-43 was the Year of the Pink Sapling. Year V-44 is the Year of Threads. Some people believe that the names of the years are an omen, or foreshadowing of what is to come - others believe this to be coincidental conjecture. In summary, ways of writing dates would be: Long: Caradras, 13th of Starsong, V-44 the Year of Threads Shortened: Caradras 13, Starsong, V-44 Shortest: 13 Starsong, V-44","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Bestiary","href":"codex/lore.html#bestiary","kind":"codex","text":"Adventurer Granson Cayle of Mill Deep in the frozen expanse of the Burning Tundra, there is said to be a spectral flame, ever-burning with icy warmth. The monsters that thrive there are enchanted with its magic, faintly glowing as they roam the wastes.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Tall Tales of the Reach: City of Serpents","href":"codex/lore.html#tall-tales-of-the-reach--city-of-serpents","kind":"codex","text":"Fire Song, Great Poet of Dalamand Through the low canyon, crimson cooked in the heat of the suns, travel merchants of death Those purveyors of bounty and baubles purloined from the coffers of old kings and royalty entombed Through the low canyon, crimson cooked they walk, to the clandestine city of Sarantara To sell their bounty to the Serpent Lords for the price of freedom and fealty Through the low canyon, crimson cooked, they die In the land of the Red Sun, elder than the depths of Her victory","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Dialogue in Sable, Edition Seven: Year of the Sapling","href":"codex/lore.html#dialogue-in-sable--edition-seven--year-of-the-sapling","kind":"codex","text":"Curte Redwind of Nischatel Great Baraid in the cold lands of Andos, currently ruled by Mountain-King Gralloch Skybane, stands as an everlasting monument of strength and will against all enemies. No invading force, in all of recorded history, have ever breached it's impregnable defenses - not Izil the Firelord in his Age of Flame, nor Horlg's Cleansing - not even the horrific, ancient Dark, which wiped out nearly all of the world's population. Great Baraid has stood firm against all. However, due to this invincibility, some politicians and court attendees believe the kingdom to be complacent, and a useless ally, even secretly silent and malicious. For what need is there for Baraid to pledge to alliance? They can withstand it all! Alas, I believe that not even the mighty and immortal Fala would be able to break through its legendary gates, and if that were the case, then my musings are irrelevant...","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Glories of Fala","href":"codex/lore.html#glories-of-fala","kind":"codex","text":"War-Historian Bernadette Laen of Fala In the Dark Age, the Great Forest was brought to ruin by the Demon Kings and Isindil burned. The burning of the world tree awoke the ancient dragon god of old, Salaseth, from his slumber in High Shasir. It is said, our great lord, Gadros, God-King of Fala, Undefeated Champion and Ruler of our Immortal Kingdom, rode the world-drake into battle, uniting the old Helfaust Clans and Invincible Legion of the mountain under a single banner against the demonic lords. The Aegis of Fala is an enchanted shield used by an ancient god in the wars against the Dark. It now rests above the Immortal Throne, alongside other artifacts of incredible power. The waters flow gently down the river Dine, to the land of Kings, mighty in their reign and lofty sat in their immortal throne, exalted by the divine The waters flow gently down the river Dine by where the blood of Eren fed the roots, of the oaks that stood and watched great empires fall and rise The waters flow gently down the river Dine, of which the muds bank, walked on by scores of voyagers, their feet leaving prints fleeting with time Named of the Lady White, by whose purity the people were graced and were from the shadow led - to be blessed by the light that shines, sparkling on the water of the river Dine, where the waters flow gently","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: History of the Ages, Volume I","href":"codex/lore.html#history-of-the-ages--volume-i","kind":"codex","text":"Kept by the Scribes of Valoris Agal's Rock in the Lost Age was used as a shamanic meeting ground for the clans of the Helfaust in their alliance against the World-Beast. Eventually, it became known as Deathrock, and the Battle of Deathrock blasted the landscape around it. Only now, in the new age, has greenery begun to return to its surroundings. Only the tip of the once mighty stone is now visible, an old survivor of war returning to the soil. The most fearsome, monstrous beasts on all of Elorae roam the plains of Awakul, the Land of Giants. The untamed wilds were once broken and scarred by ages of war, but when the Infinity washed over the landscape, life bloomed thrice over. The bravest warriors and hunters traveled to the land hearing tales of the gargantuan monsters, seeking fame and glory in the hunt. Centuries later, Dhar Awakul, the Great Kingdom of the High Hunters, stands tall over the plains - it's massive walls adorned with the skulls and bones of the primal world. ... and when the great kingdoms of Fala and Myrna united under a single banner, the Helfaust trembled in defeat. Never again would they rise to such might as in the days of The Supremacy. Out of their defeat arose the Kingdom of Morath and those who wished to conserve the ways of yore fled, never to return to the plains of their ancestral home. The Morathi have come a long way from their savage heritage. Living within the walls of a Great Kingdom, the metropolis of Morath provides comfort, order, and civilized culture to its forty thousand denizens. The Morathi still hold the strength and tenacity of their Helfaust ancestors, however, they have grown refined over decades of imperialism by the Great Kingdoms of the west. Over the ages, they have traveled far and across the realms -to many, they are seen still as dangerous, primal. Yet to most, they are greeted with open arms- either in true respect or for fear of the wrath of Fala or Myrna.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: History of the Ages: Volume II","href":"codex/lore.html#history-of-the-ages--volume-ii","kind":"codex","text":"Kept by the Scribes of Valoris Nowhere in all the world is there such natural comfort than in the great land of Syr Sable. Its idyllic region of Estic Orne is filled with rolling green fields of pastoral beauty and farm villages at peace. The mighty Fala and the great kingdoms of the world acknowledge its neutrality, and leave it alone with respect to its history.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Ilium Legendarium","href":"codex/lore.html#ilium-legendarium","kind":"codex","text":"Miriem Benedath, Historian of Aratta Tum When the Eren Dominion marched northward out of Ala Nahri on their genocidal conquest, they came out of the desert wastes upon the sparkling waters of the Kingdom of Aghoras. The Dominion, drunk on the blood of the Samafites, entered the territory and prepared for their next victory. However, they were undeniably bewildered when the cities and villages they knew to exist had vanished. The High Sorcerers of Aghoras, the magical masters of the old ways, phased the kingdom out in its entirety - as they knew they could not withstand the onslaught of the blooddrunk army. And so the Dominion continued on, with newfound caution, to meet the Golossian Kings on the northern battlefields - and ultimately, writhe in defeat at the hands of Krael, the Mighty and Immortal.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: History of the Ages: Volume III","href":"codex/lore.html#history-of-the-ages--volume-iii","kind":"codex","text":"Kept by the Scribes of Valoris After the Great Collapse came the Deep Withering, when the leylines ran dry. It was then soon after that the ashborne people enslaved the deepfolk and erected their central Kingdom of Yn'rylith. And from this atrocity birthed the unending Spawn Wars, which perpetuate a millenia later, unto this very day. It began in IV-2150, when the Mad Wizard-King of Angham, Horlg the Vile, commanded the city-gates to be chained shut, and his apothecaries to test their abominable, toxic concoctions upon his own people. In the demented horror, it is said the King waded into the market square and ensorcelled himself while basking in the screams of the afflicted. Come morning, the gates opened once again, and the infected survivors, now thralls of the King, were unleashed upon the land to spread their horrid pestilence.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: The Legend of Creation","href":"codex/lore.html#the-legend-of-creation","kind":"codex","text":"Violes Ward, Dark Age Historian and Scribe of the Concord Before the beginning of time, in the Timeless Void, there walked the entities greater than gods. These beings would be known as the Ancients, the shapers of the cosmic void. The Ancients created the innumerable stars, moons, and worlds under the direction of the designator of their work, the Prime Cosmic Lord. By the will of that Lord, the Ancients were to enact the design of an endless vision of ordered creation. The rule of the Prime Lord was absolute. And so, unseen, five ancients went, to the farthest, deepest reaches of the endless nothing, against the will of their lord. They formed a world into being - a planet - to be their own. Hidden away in the far void, the five ancients would work in secrecy. Without the watchful gaze of the Prime Cosmic Lord, the rogue ancients began to exercise their full, unrestrained power. Unto the clandestine world they had shaped, the rules of creation were broken. The Elder magics of their origin were discarded. Instead they fathomed a new framework, wholly separate in nature and boundless in potential. With magic unlimited and formless measure given form, inside a dimension within and without the mercurial abyss, they began their great work. Upon the surface of the planet, they etched and imbued their formations. The five ancients would come to learn each their own domains, and in their secret endeavor, gave themselves titles. They were named after their creations, by the language they bore into being. For the creator of dimensional space and the passage of moments, - Aghor, (Time-God), Ancient of Time and Dimensions. For the creator of the lands diverse, the designer of mountain and forest, plain and desert - Hesk (The Wild), Ancient of the Wilds. For the creator of the streams and storms, wind and ocean, - Auralon, (Without Depth or Form), Ancient of the Skies and Seas. For the creator of darkness and shadow - Sthistu, (Dark One), Ancient of the Dark. And for the creator of the first light, the suns, and the first flame in duality and balance with the Dark, - Caurobor, (Free From Shadow), Ancient of the Light. They then chose to combine their power, to discover new potential - from this endeavor they created sentient life - all manner of strange and magnificent animals and living creatures spread over the lands, skies, and seas. To protect their collaborative wonder, they birthed a defender, a new Ancient in might - Salaseth, (Great Warrior), The Ancient of War. The warden of the world would safeguard against any threat beyond their dimensional plane - a threat from the realmless expanse from whence they came, led by the Prime Cosmic Lord who was then given the name, Maegoroth, (Doom Bringer). For they knew, in the vision of Maegoroth and their Ancient kin, their covert creation was pure heresy against the order of the cosmos and would be destroyed if it were found. And so, lastly, they named their creation Elor Ae, the World Away. The Ancients each then manifested their own, private demi-realms, Elor Agh, the World of Time, Elor Hesk, the World Wild, Elor Aura, the World Without Depth, Elor Sthis, the World Dark, and Elor Boros, the World of No Shadow, to observe the passing of the ages.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: The Lost Era, Vol II, Yollonde: The Rise, Fall, and Rebirth of a Dynasty","href":"codex/lore.html#the-lost-era--vol-ii--yollonde--the-rise--fall--and-rebirth-of-a-dynasty","kind":"codex","text":"Ecleasor Fern of Caer Lire ...she inherited an empire impossible to rule. It wasn't a birthright. It was a curse.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: The Lost Era, Vol III, Nischatel: Liberation and Ascension of Nation and Goddess","href":"codex/lore.html#the-lost-era--vol-iii--nischatel--liberation-and-ascension-of-nation-and-goddess","kind":"codex","text":"Ecleasor Fern of Caer Lire The War of the Six Kings ended when the Promised Queen cast her soul into the heavens and fostered divinity within her being. Into the Ochre Depths, then, the deposed Lords were banished, and forever in that realm of doom would they remain. In the old hills, beyond the walls of the capital toward Sarthe and even northward, towards Thryne, are old ruins - once holy chapels of the Twins. Fena, and to a lesser degree, her sister, Nadra, were worshiped throughout Nischatel during the reign of the Dalamand Monarchy, but when it was ended by the Six Kings, religious freedom was banished. Only after our bountiful Queen-Goddess Sar Sareen liberated us, have we begun to return to these shrines of our old faith, and rekindle what was lost.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Megana, our Goddess of Arcane Secrets and Lore Profound","href":"codex/lore.html#megana--our-goddess-of-arcane-secrets-and-lore-profound","kind":"codex","text":"Vereen Starheart When the Godform destroyed Aghor, they absorbed his soul thrice - Rathalon, Malicator, and Elraim each. However, it is understood that Aghor's death sent ripples cascading into the Infinity enshrouding our world. Thus it is such that in deep places untouched by common hands and old ruins that once held might, there pools hidden power. These concentrations may harbor secrets of the arcane, fonts of isolated magic and memory, or fragments of supernal knowledge once held by the highest God of yore. One rising sorceress, enamored by this truth, grew a following of acolytes drawn by her passion for the discovery of ancient wisdom. In youth, her name was Megana, and by the hands of her and her scholars through devotion alone, found divinity.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Military History of Nybei III","href":"codex/lore.html#military-history-of-nybei-iii","kind":"codex","text":"The Scribes of San Zai Of the five great nations of Far Nybei, the elevated and war-torn High Kaiden with their unyielding warriors alone defended the land against the final onslaught of the Cursed Army of Balos. The greatest of these heroes became known as the Cursebreakers, and their legendary order has since defended the five nations for eight generations. (However, we must add, the Venerable Jiku of Otan, of Kanta Masa, believes that the Curse of Balos was not destroyed, and that Kaiden now carries it in secret. This remains a mere myth.) Some say that even the might of the legendary and immortal Fala would not be enough to defeat the Legion of Kaiden, led by the Great Shogura Isai IV. Perhaps, Fala feigns disinterest in light of this truth.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Musings of an Old Mason","href":"codex/lore.html#musings-of-an-old-mason","kind":"codex","text":"Jann Hardwind In Wonders, by Wise Tamilan, I read that deep in the Great Green Domain lies the Archwood, \"the truest manifestation of the grandeur and colossal magnificence of our natural world.\" How often we forget about the wild existence outside our city walls as we live and labor in smoke filled corridors and cobbled streets. How quickly I would I run far away, to exotic realms on foreign shores, how quick I would, if only I were young again. Alas, I am not... my lot was that of a brickmason, and a laborer I will die. In another life...","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Mysteries of Old","href":"codex/lore.html#mysteries-of-old","kind":"codex","text":"Scholar Han Farrah From Sudal's Rainbow Coast, set out to sail as far east as east goes, then keep going. Beyond all distance a sane sailor would travel, you'll eventually reach the mist covered land of Far Nybei, where the narrow mountains stretch toward the sky, the wilds are untamed yet in balance, and the people are peaceful, yet mightier than one could believe. The Vespertine Keepers guard their extravagant palace filled with magical secrets. Master Renraen says under the guidance of the one who they call the Vesper Lord, they perform great rituals to Astar, the dead god of the twilight. The Warrior-Monks of Nixil Valley practice the sacred ways of an ancient master said to have found inner peace. Throughout the ages, deep underground, they have followed the doctrines and principles of utmost duty, the inseparable marriage of body and mind, and unwavering perseverance. However, some believe them to be hiding some unseen sinistry. Tales tell of a paradise tucked away in the farthest, unreachable place of all the realms. In all the days from the creation of the planes to the ravages of cosmic war, it has remained pristine and has never experienced destruction. It is only spoken of in detail by one culture - the Highborne of Essen Revir write of it as 'an elysian glade of gentle creeks, cresting waterfalls, singing birds, and gardens of immortal flora that are ever-touched by early-mornings dawnlight'. Over beyond the Plains of Autumn, built into a mountainside high above the Three Rapids is a chapel with no known path of entrance. Long ago it was called Minfidium, and served as a home to faithless heretics. It is unknown what lies within now.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: The Nature of the Divines","href":"codex/lore.html#the-nature-of-the-divines","kind":"codex","text":"Barthemael Arnast, High Priest of Thryne It is known that divinity is a thing that within every soul can be achieved. Whether thou be a child birthed with royal blood coursing through thine veins, or a modest farmer, whose hands tendeth the seeds and soil. For thou knowest the length of life may be extended through heroic virtue, or pure might - it is of our nature, akin. It is thus, that those of legend whomst found within themselves the strength of will to manifest some remnant of the fractured Infinity, after the death of God, become worshiped, or feared, in kind.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: On Greenmen","href":"codex/lore.html#on-greenmen","kind":"codex","text":"Abadeen Melmor, Felinellan Scholar The savage calling of the wilds beckons those with wild souls within. To leave the comfortable confines of wrought stone and iron to rejoin the wooded ways is a life that only some wish to embrace. However, the embrace of the forests is only the beginning - the practice of Green magic in kind, is the calling of the Greenmen. Green magic, the magic of the Wild Gods, can grant the secrets of life itself - from the rejuvenating waters of springs and glades, the life force of the wildlife, or the perseverance of the deeprooted trees that ever enshroud its harmony. Though harmonious always, it is not... Greenfolk may often be of peace and tranquility, but, as the suns and the moons rise and fall, so is the duality of peace and violence. Those of the savage ways, touched by the carnivorous taint of the Cursed Hunt - the path of blood and carnage, are savage Greenmen who seek to despoil and feast upon the innocent. Often, Greenmen, savage or peaceful, might practice Beast magic, changing their bodies or transmorphing their forms into that of non-humanoids. Some might by cursed with Blood magic, tainted further by the curse to devolve into bloodcrazed monsters, such as in cases of lycanthropy. Many would not know of these facts, but alas they do not venture too far into the sylvan domains...","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Peoples, Cultures, Ways of Life","href":"codex/lore.html#peoples--cultures--ways-of-life","kind":"codex","text":"Remis Hanlaud, Professor of the Folk at the Valorum The Rose Knights protect the beauty of the Singing Glade deep in the Elen Asvan heartland. Many unwise wayfarers from the lands of Morath and foolish traders from Tumunz often 'explore' the lands near the Glade without permission. They do not suspect the wrath of the armored guardians, nor for their corpses to soon fertilize the fields of flowers, forbidden to outsiders. The central region of the continent Essen Revir is ruled by the Great Kingdom of the Highborne, Elen Asva. It is bordered by the Great Kingdom of Tumunz in the west, and by the Great Kingdom of Morath to the East. The capital itself is deep inland, surrounded by bountiful forests and glades inhabited by all manner of people. You can see the influence of Elen Asva in much of the surrounding architecture and culture in its refinement. Elen Asva is in alliance with the mighty and immortal Kingdom of Fala, as are most of the Great Kingdoms. The Arrows of Andos are elite rangers who patrol the snow-covered crags of the Andosan Rise against monstrous ice creatures. The Rangers of Roq are a squadron of archers hailing out of the peaks of the Roq Mountains who are known to command great flying mounts. The sethian are a strong folk, native to the fiery peaks of Shasir. They have spread far and wide, showing their resilience, inhabiting every climate across the realm from the Frostlands to the jungles of Canthir. Their societies are deeply steeped in tradition and they often worship gods of old. For a sethian, family is all and to be ostracized is a fate worse than death.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Philosophical Thoughts and Commentary","href":"codex/lore.html#philosophical-thoughts-and-commentary","kind":"codex","text":"Elisse Ward, Custodian of the Great Archive in Valoris And as the land was reformed and Elorae built into true glory, new kingdoms arose to monolithic heights unseen since the mythic age of the Infinite Throne. These came to be known as The Great Kingdoms, dwarfing the estimated size of Old Seltandor, Ancient Argavos of the North, and Ajin'tatel in the Primordial Greenrealm. Each Great Kingdom was built on a legendary discovery such as The Great Glass City, unparalleled might such as the indomitable and immortal Fala, or such incomparable wealth as Tumunz. Yet there is one Great Kingdom that scholars such as I are confounded by, whose origins are steeped in mystery, and whose very foundations are unknown. That is of course, the sky-city of Adventus Eternal, ruled by The Ageless Lord, known only to appear before great catastrophe as a grim omen.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: The Rites","href":"codex/lore.html#the-rites","kind":"codex","text":"Translated from ancient Nybeian texts circa II-900, unknown authors To craft an empire, one needeth devote thyself to rule a people, one needeth command thyself foremost To conquer, one needeth relinquish thy burdens, to destroy thine enemy, one needeth save thine people An empire commanded by devotion shant ever fall to meek opposition or be shadowed by the Darkness A dynasty commanded by devotion, shall ruled be - by thy son's son and by thy daughter's daughter, forevermore Such is the way of Kaiden I, first ruler, king of kings, emperor of emperors","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Twin Fates: Lore of the Celestial Sands","href":"codex/lore.html#twin-fates--lore-of-the-celestial-sands","kind":"codex","text":"Compiled by the South Dawn Conclave In alabaster sands, beyond glimmering seas of forbidden emeralds, grassed desert marshes bestow wondrous life into wondrous blooms, great and resplendent Of diligence and grace the pained tenders dwell, duty-bound and resolute In forbiddance of those harvesters of power Blessed and cursed, duty and bane, betwixt In the white eyes of Sura, and in the black eyes of Sana","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Seasons","href":"codex/lore.html#seasons","kind":"codex","text":"Hollan Meer of Asva The gentle autumn breeze blew in and filled the countryside with a welcomed warmth. The foliage was gold as prosperity and the wine flowed like the Sullen River.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Warnings of the Blooded Prophet","href":"codex/lore.html#warnings-of-the-blooded-prophet","kind":"codex","text":"The whispers in the Dark get only louder. They call to something lingering. Something old. We must listen, lest we lose ourselves to the delights of the new world. The horns sound once more in the deep.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Worldly Stories","href":"codex/lore.html#worldly-stories","kind":"codex","text":"Scholar Em Tirne The Old Irildar Ruins are lost deep under the burning desert sands. Stories that tell of its history say it was once a city that was destroyed by a tyrant pyromancer, others, that it was conquered and razed by the fabled Houses of Vald. Yet some few scholars give credence to the writings that say it was swallowed by the night itself and its people were cursed to forever haunt the sands as restless wraiths. The land of Samafi is an exceptionally diverse place despite it's heat, rolling sand dunes, and landmarks of colossal bleached-bone remains. The eclectic Sages of Preste claim there to be a sprawling underground realm the size of the desert itself. They say it to be 'a land of ice and frost, as if some ancient god were attempting to rise out of the depths and freeze the surface world'. The great countryside of Voh is like another realm compared to the bustle of civilization in a Great Kingdom. With a days travel through the countryside, you'll see wooded glades, crashing waterfalls, open croplands, and rest in some of the most delightful taverns you'll ever visit. The farmers and commoners that inhabit the region are humble, strong, simple folk that will welcome you with open arms. The briny shores of Vnoll are ever blasted by tumultuous waves of the bottomless sea. The coast dwellers speak of terrors of the deep, shared nightmares, and storms that make lifelong sailors disappear forever. The tale of the Betrayer is one that finds itself entwined within the tales of the Godform and in old world history. Often, the stories are told to children as a wise lesson, as proverbs found within cultures across the realm, or sung before tavern hearths in versions of \"The Betrayer's Lament.\" In the story, one theme is that of a fallen saint - a good and just man who falls to temptation and regret, another is on the traitorous betrayal of love and trust. The Godslayer spoke of the dangers of the rift, but the Keepers of the Source did not heed his counsel. Though, when the day came and the raw infinity poured forth consuming all, tearing them from their seats of power and rule, it is said their screams from within the chamber betrayed their stubborn pride.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: Words of Promise","href":"codex/lore.html#words-of-promise","kind":"codex","text":"Fanastan, the Poet of Greenbank Far beyond the borders of those dark places where lingers carnage and hate Beyond the reach of the corrupt and raucous - the politics of civility Beyond the suffering and death that once befell our people We live on, fed by the seeds in soil planted by heroes past Out of the despair of oblivion, into the dawnlight anew","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Lore: The Wretched Tragedy of Elraim","href":"codex/lore.html#the-wretched-tragedy-of-elraim","kind":"codex","text":"Lady Filibeth In the echoing silence of thy city, a cacophony of abandonment, thou sitteth, forlorn - a lord of nothing. Thine empire, to ash, crumbled as dust in the wind. Thine people with pain and hatred, scattered across the land. Solitude, in pursuit of mankind's liberation. Even now, thou hold'st the power to dethrone the Divines thrice over. Yet, without want, it is said power is without meaning. So by thy kin, is thy passion returned. Nurtured by thy tripartite soul, thou find'st want. Doubtless, to the epicenter of yore, tread'st thee. An attempt to unbefoul thy tarnished legacy. Thus, as it was from Third to Fourth, so shall it be, from Fourth to Fifth. Infinity. Casteth upon the world, by the sacrifice of a great and wretched lord. And so the world is saved. Forever on, shall thy stories be written. In the land promised, by the sprouting of a sapling pink. Thy roots which runneth deep, thy veins from which thou bleed, by thy leaves, shalt we guided be, life, given by thee, sanctuary. Thou shalt guarded be, by thine children, eternally, for we followeth their decree, so thee, shalt darkness, never see.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Magics: How does magic work?","href":"codex/magics.html#how-does-magic-work","kind":"codex","text":"There are many types of magic, however, there are two fundamental frameworks: The Cosm and The Infinite, followed by divisions of the frameworks that can be separated into different magical arts. The spells and effects that are caused by these magical arts can be organized into eight magical 'schools', which will be described. The Cosm is the magical framework of the Timeless Void. There is only one division pulled from the framework, with no true subdivisions, Elder magic. Elder magic, also referred to as Cosmic magic, is ancient and almost extinct on Elorae - it is the raw energy used by the beings known as Ancients that created substance out of the void, the shapers of the cosmos. Additionally, it has the innate ability to dominate minds, and psychically manipulate it’s surroundings, including things composed from the Infinite. There are instances of spellcasters able to naturally manipulate the Cosm from birth in this fashion, however it is extraordinarily rare. These people are known as Psions. Psi magic is not a subdivision, rather, it is an art of Elder magic manipulation. For all but the most elite sorcerer, or those without a natural-born gift, it is entirely out of reach. To the common eye, Elder magic looks primarily black with some traces of purple, blue and dark gold. A sorcerer peers into a landscape infused with Elder magic. The Infinite is the magical framework birthed into being by the leader of the Ancient creators of Elorae, Aghor. Infinity magic is the magic pulled from the framework that may be used in any way the one harnessing it wishes, if sufficiently powerful. The Infinite was created by Aghor to divorce their heretical creation of Elorae from their kin and origin. The Infinite suffuses everything and can be divided into many different magics. In high concentrations, the Infinite is dangerous. There are places in the world where the Infinite coalesces - the most well known being The Confluence, and the old leylines of the deep Nexus. To the common eye, raw Infinite magic is vibrant green. Often, the Infinite may be referred to as The Infinity, or other names, that colloquially refer to the same magical framework. Most prominently there are five divisions of The Infinite. First, there are the two immaterial properties of Temporality (Time) and Dimensionality (Space). Second, there are the material properties of The Dark, The Light, and The Primal. Immaterial properties deal with the non-physical, such as spacial distortion and the manipulation of time, while material properties deal with the physical; physical light and darkness, souls, the elements, and living beings. The Dark and Light are relatively straightforward - they are two sides of the same coin, and a pure duality - one may not exist without the other. The Primal is multifaceted and constitutes a vast range of magical arts. Many flavors of Dark magic exist, and though historically it has been infamously used for evil, nothing about the Dark is inherently malicious. A Dark magician may revere the night and the calm that it brings or the cold of the shadow against the heat of the blazing sun. At its best, Dark is the energy of calm, peace, and respite. At its worst, Dark is the energy of fear and horror. Dark magic can further be broken down into divisions such as Shadow magic, the art of controlling shadows and warding against the encroachment of light, Death magic, the art of harming or ending life, and Black magic, the esoteric arts that deal with negative energy - a black magician may agitate fears, anxieties, or doubts. Many flavors of Light magic exist, and again, though historically it has been used for good, nothing about the Light is inherently benevolent. A Light magician may revere the day and the motion that it brings or the warmth of the sun against the cold of shadow. At its best, Light is the energy of warmth, hope, and life. At its worst, Light is the energy of oppression and blind devotion. Light magic can further be broken do","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Magics: How is magic learned and accessed?","href":"codex/magics.html#how-is-magic-learned-and-accessed","kind":"codex","text":"The Infinite can be accessed by anyone, as every soul is connected to it. One’s aptitude for manipulating the Infinite and bending it to their specific desires in different ways is based on their innate abilities (spellcasting ability). Those with gifted intellect may learn how to manipulate the Infinite through study, gathering a deep understanding of its arcane structure through practice. Those who follow the wisdom of a Divine may read religious texts to gain knowledge on how to shape the Infinite. Others may have a heightened, natural gift for molding the Infinite. Manipulating the Infinite taxes the spellcaster and temporarily frays one’s soul. As souls are linked to the Infinite, when one manipulates the Infinite their soul interacts with it. Too much spellcasting will weaken your soul until you can no longer manipulate the Infinity. One must rest before further manipulation. An assassin siphons the soul of his victim using Death magic.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Magics: Does the Infinite have a will?","href":"codex/magics.html#does-the-infinite-have-a-will","kind":"codex","text":"The Infinite is not intelligent, nor does it have a will of its own. It is simply the aetheric structure that composes all matter on Elorae.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Magics: Is there a limit to the potential of magic?","href":"codex/magics.html#is-there-a-limit-to-the-potential-of-magic","kind":"codex","text":"There is no ceiling to the potential of Elder magic or Infinity magic. Elder magic was used by the Ancients to shape innumerable planets in the Celestial Abyss. The Infinite magic has been displayed to be boundless in potential, as showcased by the castings of ‘Infinity’ at the turning of the ages.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Magics: How are spellcasters perceived?","href":"codex/magics.html#how-are-spellcasters-perceived","kind":"codex","text":"The perception of spellcasters varies greatly based on region and magical practice. Life magic is generally well regarded while Death magic is generally reviled. Black magic is generally outlawed in major cities, while White magic is welcomed. Certain clans and types of peoples may put different value on their spellcasters - some may be heads of society and cultural leaders, while others may be shunned or exiled. There are certain places well-known for their focus on certain Magics. The Great Forests of Hesk are the ancestral home of druidism and Green magics. The Circle of Sorcery in central Ilium Aghor carry the legacy of the ancient Aghorians and their mastery over Temporality and Dimensionality. The Cult of the Aspected Flame in the desert city of Sarantara are venerated wielders of Fire magic. Many more examples await discovery.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Magics: Why haven’t powerful spellcasters taken over the world, or destroyed it?","href":"codex/magics.html#why-haven-t-powerful-spellcasters-taken-over-the-world--or-destroyed-it","kind":"codex","text":"They have, historically. The greatest sorcerers with the largest souls have ascended to Divinity after the death of Aghor at the hands of the Godform. Sorcerers who have ascended to divinity and became Divines have amassed religious followings and have caused great catastrophe such as Horlg’s Cleansing, scorched lands to cinder such as in 'The Burning’ of Izil the Firelord, and have conquered entire continents such as in The Savagery. Even before the reach of Divinity, great wizards have done great and terrible things, and before that the Draconic Empire lorded over meek humanity with elemental mastery. However, the deeds of the Divines are the tales of legend, and though legends exist, the vast majority of spellcasters will never reach Divinity. Just as there are evil powers that use magic for destruction, there are benevolent powers that use magic for restoration. The ages are a growing history of this dichotomy. A great dragon incinerates an army using Fire magic.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Souls: What is a soul?","href":"codex/souls.html#what-is-a-soul","kind":"codex","text":"On Elorae, ‘The Infinite’ is the magical framework that suffuses all matter and is responsible for physics and the passage of time. You can think of the Infinite as an invisible force that is within everything, animate or inanimate. From the Infinite, the shapers of Elorae - the Rogue Ancients - created everything. Included in their creations were sentient life. Sentience: conscious or responsive awareness of surroundings, capable of sensory perception and feeling Sentience brings forth a particular occurrence from the Infinite. Where sentience is found, a soul is created, and without a soul, there is no sentience. As the Infinite invisibly suffuses all of the world, so too does a soul invisibly suffuse a sentient creature. This means that all manner of animals, monsters, and creatures that are sentient carry a soul. When a creature dies, it’s soul is returned to the Infinite. Many cultures have different explanations for the afterlife. The art of Spirit magic attempts to connect to the Infinite to rebuild and commune with the faded souls of the deceased - in this art, restructured souls in the Infinite are called spirits and interact as the spells of the Spirit magician conduct. Souls and the Infinite are inextricably linked and tethered. This means that all souls are technically connected to each other, too. Souls can grow in vitality from how they interact with other souls. For example, a king who has the hearts of his people will have a richer soul than an outland hermit. Essentially, the kings soul has been empowered by the connection created with the souls of his people. His people think of him regularly and may perform acts in service to him, which causes his soul to gain vigor. On the contrary, if he were to be reviled by many, feared, or responsible for the death of many, his soul would also gain vigor. Another example may be that of a group of heroes who slay a monster that is terrorizing a village. The monster has a vigorous soul, as it’s reign of terror is affecting many other souls. When it dies, it’s vigorous soul is returned to the Infinite, and the souls of the heroes grow in kind. Again, a soul grows in vitality from how they interact with other souls. Souls are directly connected with the aging of a creature. The soul, at a baseline, naturally withers away (a lifespan of birth and death). The more rich and vigorous the soul, the longer ones life may be. However, this does not mean everyone who leads a great and cherished life, or every villain who oppresses others may live forever. It takes an extraordinary amount of soul vigor to extend life far beyond mortal limits (however, Death and Life magic are commonly practiced arts of manipulating the vigor of a soul.) Also, the vigor of ones soul generally affects their magical aptitude, strength, and physical attributes. A powerful soul is able to magically manipulate the Infinite and perform feats of strength beyond the abilities of a common soul. Soul vigor is passed down through bloodlines, but only slightly. While the daughter of a Fire mage may have the aptitude for Fire magic above another without a magical parent, the daughter still must practice and learn on their own to foster their potential. Vigor through lineage does not guarantee every generation to be as vigorous or equivalent to the last - each individual still must grow their vigor naturally, they just might be more adept to the arts of their forebears.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Souls: What is divinity?","href":"codex/souls.html#what-is-divinity","kind":"codex","text":"A soul may become so bountiful in vigor that it may undergo the process of ascension. When a soul ascends, its connection with the Infinite becomes unimaginably powerful. An ascended soul is referred to as a Divine soul, thus divinity. Those who have ascended are referred to as Divines, Deities, or Gods. A Divine soul is eternal and unaffected by the natural process of age. They are simply too vigorous too wither. When confronted with the path of ascension, it is an innate feeling - an intuitive choice that is indescribable to those who have not experienced it. Ascension to divinity fundamentally transforms ones soul and physical being - many emotions and mortal thoughts become lost, and ones connection to the physical world becomes permanently altered. One capable of ascension may choose to deny it, and live and die as a lesser soul without becoming a Divine - such as the Great Lady Demethine of the Fourth Era. It should go without saying, ascension is phenomenally rare. A divine may begin to manipulate and control the Infinite in ways beyond the comprehension of lesser souls. One ascends to Divinity based on their legendary feats or influence over other souls. Nearly all Gods were practitioners of certain magical arts or figures of indomitable might, widespread praise, or rampant disgust. To lesser souls, Divines are gods. Divines are generally worshiped throughout Elorae as deities, with each specific Divine having a domain that embodies their tenets and masteries. For example, Sar Sareen, Divine of renewal, purity, and clarity, was an exalted queen of her land responsible for defeating the manipulative and deceitful Six-Kings, and ending their reign of clouded terror. A Divine may receive prayers and services in their name, which they may respond to. If a Divine oversteps and begins to inflict harm upon the world, it is common that other Divines may attempt to stop them - creating a tenuous and ever-shifting balance. Divine souls cannot be destroyed by mortal means. Only the power of a Divine soul may inflict harm on another Divine soul. A lesser soul simply cannot naturally harm a Divine soul. The Divines are sometimes classified by the magical divisions that they inherit, and may be referred to as Dark Divines, Light Divines, and Primal Divines (sometimes also called Dark, Light, and Primal Divinities). Religions may also be referred to colloquially as 'divinities', for example, it may be said “She is a follower of the Divinity of Sar Sareen”. However, exceptions exist, and some Divines that inherit traits of mixed magics may be classified as either Dark, Light, and Primal Divines regardless. Divines that may embody Temporality and Dimensionality are generally just referred to as Divines without a descriptor.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Souls: What is the difference between a Divine and an Ancient?","href":"codex/souls.html#what-is-the-difference-between-a-divine-and-an-ancient","kind":"codex","text":"The Ancients were the shapers of the cosmos and creators of Elorae. The Ancients existed outside of the Infinite, in fact, they created it. For this reason, the Ancients did not have souls akin to the souls found within the creatures of modern day Elorae, they had something greater - among creation scholars, they are known as the Souls of Creation. The ancients were ephemeral entities that had the power of true omnipotence and omnipresence. Only other Ancients could interact on the same level of being and block the actions of another. However, they were individuals and ultimately did still have an individual essence, however incomprehensible it may be. Aghor, the last-surviving leader of the Rogue Ancients who created the world, was brought forth and destroyed by the Godform in a climactic, catastrophic engagement which ended the third age. The Godform believed Aghor, who was then known as ‘God’, was actively suppressing the Infinite and keeping the true potential of mortals subdued. In truth, Aghor was doing this, for reasons unexplained. The essence of Aghor, known as the God Soul was split tripartite among the Godform (thus the origin of their title). And so, the Infinite was no longer bound by its creator and souls were forever after granted the potential of ascension. The Godform is not a single entity. It is the title of the three legendary lords that slayed the god of the world and divided that ‘soul’ amongst themselves. Divine souls hold a fraction of the power held by the Ancients and they can never reach the same level. As Divines are gods to mortals, Ancients are gods to Divines. Some extremely rare beings don’t fall into either category, have unique classifications and powers, and still rival the strength of Divines. These wondrous and terrible powers are out there, waiting to be discovered, feared, or worshiped.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Souls: Heirarchy of power:","href":"codex/souls.html#heirarchy-of-power","kind":"codex","text":"Ancients Divine Souls Lesser Souls (Mortals)","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Atlas","href":"atlas.html","kind":"atlas","text":"Where are we? Elorae, \"Elor Ae\" meaning \"The World Away\" in the language of the Ancients. The world is an old one, a lone planet adrift in a cosmic void. Over the ages, the world has seen the ravages of magics and countless wars, the rise and fall of empires, and the growth and decay of magnificent landscapes. Landmarks from days past lie between great kingdoms, shining seas crash upon breezy coasts, and within caves and ruins creep things better left unknown. What does the world look like? On Elorae, in the Fifth Era, there are nine continental landmasses and a massive subterrane underneath. Please use the tabs above to learn a brief overview of each continent.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Cosm","href":"atlas/cosm.html","kind":"atlas","text":"Cosm The southernmost continent of Elorae. Cosm once was a verdant, tropical land, teeming with exotic wildlife. However, devastation by magical dragonfire turned the bountiful landscape into an arid waste of crystallized mana, known as the Gem Waste. Out of this waste, civilization still sprung, and desert cities were constructed on economies of gemstone trade. Magic crystals, much of what the endless grounds of the wasteland became, are highly coveted as resources for energy throughout the continent. Rarely, export out of Cosm across the northern sea occurs. One may find Cosm crystals enchanting the armaments of Awakuli High Hunters, adorning Highborne walls in Elen Asva, or on the tradewinds of the Sea of Gold into Tumunz. As you move south into the lands of Ur, the crystals become more infrequent than in the Gem Waste, giving way to lands of total dust and sand, with sparsely populated cities and villages. Many are built into cliffside caves where mountains rise - or beneath the dunes entirely, in an effort to avoid vicious mana-infused sandstorms that blow from the north. Very rarely, oases might show their lush greenery within the acrid region, but are quickly defended by the people who claim them. Old cities lie buried under the wastes of Jud'hin. Untold riches and history are forever covered in sand. Toward the southern coast, the deserts turn to icy gravel, and tiny-but-dense forests begin to form. The storms become roaring mana-blizzards and snows fall on hilly terrain. The coastline is largely inaccessible, as massive sheer cliffs stare down onto the endless frozen expanse of the Forbidden Sea. Scholars believe Cosm to have been much larger than it is today, and posit that the cliffs before the sea are a barrier to exploration - the possibility of forgotten, sunken lands frozen beneath the ice. Common foods in most regions of Cosm consist of ur-leaves, a very common gray, thick-leafed plant that is ground into a meaty pulp. Salt is in abundance, with many saying it is the best tasting salt in the world, as well as gemwater, a highly nutritious liquid that is thought to be perspirated into the ground from the mana of crystals. Around oases, roseberries and gorrups often bloom and are treated as sweet and sour delicacies. The indigenous people of Cosm are known as the Cosmai. Cosmai were traditionally bronze of skin and dark haired, but millenia of exposure to latent mana and the crystal wastes has altered their nature. Modern Cosmai have a range of diverse skin tones and hair colors, pertaining to the lands of their birth. On average, northern Cosmai are generally darker of skin and southern Cosmai are generally paler of skin. Often, uncommon colors get passed down through skin pigment - eye color, and hair color too - a product of the mana exposure. Culturally, Cosmai have customs unlike many places of the world, most important of which is slavery, which is common in the brutal crystal mines that are ever-present throughout the lands. Two travelers finally reach their destination after traveling through the treacherous Ashap-Ur Valley. Cosmai are the only race of people with an innate proclivity toward Psi magic, harboring more Psions amongst their lands than the entirety of Elorae combined. Some argue that this is due to generational alteration by the strange crystals, others, that it is of an ageless, hidden history - the mark of Ancient design. In truth, the name of the continent shares itself with that of the framework of the Elder magic. It is unknown which was named first.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Essen Revir","href":"atlas/essen-revir.html","kind":"atlas","text":"Essen Revir The mid-southern continent of Elorae. From the golden coasts of the west, to the marshes and plains of the east, and to the magnificent singing glades in between, Essen Revir boasts a diverse range of regions and cultures. There are six main regions on the continent. Of gold sands and sun-scorched coasts, the nation of Tumunz sits proudly in a great lush oasis known as Tum Saral. There, in the exotic oasis, palmed goldtrees blow in the warm winds as the sparkling blue waters lap on the sandy shores. The capital of the nation, Tumunzir, is famously known for its unmatched wealth and is the ancestral home of Larellan the Golden. Tum Masa is home to the worlds largest banks, where it is said their coffers of treasure are endless. Many merchants travel to and from the Golden Coast of Tumunz from all over to engage in dreams of mecantile. Tumunz is known for its many agricultural exports such as the deliciously sweet golden apples, tart tumfruit, and the exotic arupo. Tum Masa sits proudly under the golden glow of the suns. South of Tumunz is the region of Jatar, a lush landscape of warm tropics and ridged rainforests. Most of the civilization of Jatar rests on the coast, known as the Jatar Strand. Its largest city, Denthe, offers safety within its walls from raiders and pirates off the coast, however, there is no primary principality in Jatar. Rather, the seaside villages engage in trade amongst themselves as well as Tumunz. Trade with the eastern land of Elen Asva is limited, as the journey is a long challenge through narrow roads winding under canopy of dangerous wetforests. Jatar's main food source is nutritious coastal kelp that is farmed extensively up and down the shores, which is made into the highly-filling, cultural staple, jat. Ages ago, Jatar was said to be a military power of the greatest naval fleet of all the lands. However, that glory is an old history now, and the days of the fearsome Den'jatar sea-kings are long past. In the middle of the continent, Elen Asva reigns as undisputed superpower of Essen Revir. The inner land is one of utmost beauty, containing lush flower plains, thick and colorful forests, and deep valleys with extraordinary waterfalls. Food in Asva is found all over, in abundance - lavender truffles, salletgrain, bluerose palms, and the cultural staple, lifelilies. Throughout, many incredible cities rise from the bountiful landscape such as Orian Ras and Eles Jaen, showcasing the long history of the nation after being rebuilt from the ashes of the second age. Elen Asva belongs to the Highborne and none other. In ages past, the Highborne warred with the Helfaust clans of the barren east and came close to being destroyed forever. In truth, the Highborne lost. However, after allegiant reinforcements from the kingdoms of Fala and Myrna, the hordes of Morath were crushed, to never rise again to the fervor seen in the days of The Supremacy. The village of Sesana lies tucked within the majestic forests of inner Asva, not far from Orian Ras. The Highborne are a race of proud, beautiful people blessed with symmetrical, fine, and unblemished features. The Highborne are fair of skin, pale of hair, and well-proportioned. Compared to most others, the perfection of the Highborne body, hair, and skin, may seem unnatural and uncomforting. Outside of Elen Asva, Highborne are often seen as out-of-place. Some enemies of the Highborne of Elen Asva claim that their incredible innate beauty is a disguise or obscurement of their true, sinister nature. To the east are the great open lands of Morath. As the forests of Asva open to grassy highlands, the rolling hills of the wide Than Plateau stretch for hundreds of miles. The plateau then steeply drops eastward, becoming the lowlands known as Morath. Historically, this territory was occupied by the Morathi, a tribal people who are more widely known as \"Helfaust\" - originally a derogatory term dating back to the Vingian Empire of the first age, roughly meaning \"Bl","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Far Nybei","href":"atlas/far-nybei.html","kind":"atlas","text":"Far Nybei The isolated continent, far to the east of Essen Revir and far to the west of Ilium Aghor. Nybei has five distinct regions which have long histories of intermittent allegiance and warfare. The legends of Nybei are old, with many of its lands being first settled in the dawning of the first age - particularly the legendary High Kaiden. The continent is commonly seen as exceedingly distant, and its geopolitical status is generally ignored by the Great Kingdoms of the world. Leina, a village in the lower Nasani region, sits on the Crystal River under a clear sky. Beginning from the North, Kanta Masa is a fertile, lush lowland with windy coasts and arable farmland. Its bordering waters are dotted with an archipelago called the Nine Isles, where there exist centuries old sea-villages of modest farmers and sailors. Kanta Masa, though it is a relatively peaceful coastal countryside, has been the victim of vicious ocean disasters. Historically, Kanta Masa was one of the hardest hit by the Flooding, a catastrophic tsunami event that also decimated other coasts of Hesk and Essen Revir. Away from the coast, inland, lie great swaths of farmland, generating huge amounts of rice, potatoes, root vegetables, sugar, and textiles, all of which are also used for export to the south. Kanta Masa does not have a centralized government, rather its villages are represented by elders that regularly hold representative council in the sole city of Nasan. The states of Jai Se begin south of Xinxe's Divide, a mountain range with one high-traffic highway running through, known for its clogged border, militarized by both north and south countries. The country of Jai Se is made up of twelve individual states that have their own laws and strictures. The higher level of structured government and drawn political borders of Jai Se lies in stark contrast to the relaxed, liberal nature of Kanta Masa. Historically, this has caused many cultural differences which lead to disputes and conflict between the two regions. Jai Se is largely temperate, with many rivers cutting through the land providing fertile farmgrounds. Quarries in the country provide a lucrative industry of stonecutting and masonry for architecture, and very importantly, salt however, this also has led to the enslavement of weaker states - causing bitter hatred between borders, or the lesser states' total annexation. States such as Hujin and Fou have opulent, sprawling palace cities built for their Lords, while the people of Wan are objectified and live in shambling, resource-starved \"dirt-cities\". Old ruins of the ceremonial grounds beneath Ganto quarry, are still preserved and respected by the Fou nobility. South of Jai Se, beyond the Great Bell Valley, rises the jagged lands of Ai Ishi. Many believe Ai Ishi to have been created by the Painter Gods, a pantheon unique to the region that explains the way the valleys carve through the region, overgrown with trees and vegetation bright in color, as a painters' palette. The lowest point in the land, far below sea level but bordered by hills and hanging forests, is Shune's Basin. A well-guarded, holy site to many Ishin in reverence, and named after Shune, Goddess of Laments. Many other points of interest are named after her, marking her importance to the Ishin people - also showcasing an interesting dichotomy between the melancholic, sorrowful nature of the goddess with the vibrant, bountiful landscape that she represents. Ishin live in large villages and small cities that dot the varied region. They mostly subsist of sweet diets, high in fruit due to its abundance throughout. Pinpins, nuri, cherries, kakano, and pinkberries are all extensively farmed and enjoyed, and are traded with the rich merchants of Jai Se during the annual ringing-of-the-bells. The farthest south of Ai Ishi begins to drastically ascend in elevation, the high hills becoming a great mountain range known as the Cloud Pearl mountains - named so because of the light that bounce","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Hesk","href":"atlas/hesk.html","kind":"atlas","text":"Hesk The Great Green Domain of Hesk lays east of Essen Revir and west of Ilium Aghor. Much of Hesk has yet been unplumbed, resting protected and secluded from the engines of progress present throughout Elorae. The continent of Hesk takes its name from the Ancient of the Wild, Hesk. It was also once called 'Hesk Felinel (The Forest of Hesk)' in ages past and is still colloquially known as the Greenrealm, the Great Wild, the Archwood, and the Great Forest. Generally, common opinion toward Hesk is that it is a far-away, mystical place, where commonfolk are forbidden to go. Travel by boat is rare, there are no bustling cities to visit, and its forests are both revered and feared for their mystery. However, it is said that there are small villages of inhabitants - the people of the green domain are simply called Hesk-kin, or Heskan. An ancient sylvan village rests within the boughs of totem oaks, inundated in Green magic. The Great Forest is separated by scholars into three distinct regions., the north, central, and south forests. The northern forest is known as the Great Northern Wood. The furthest tip of the continent is rather cold, with evergreens abundant like white pines and totem firs. There are said to be dire bears gargantuan in size, and snow stags that tower over the tallest men. As you approach the center mass of the land, you enter the legendary Archwood. Aptly named, all other forests pale in comparison to the sheer scale of the Arch-trees, who tower into the clouds. Some great apothecaries have written that the bark of these godly trees can be an ingredient in immortality elixirs. It is thought that the Archwood was where the Ancient, Hesk, was born, or possibly where he was laid to rest - and the trees grew out of his spirit as it infused the soil. In the greater south, the green realm expands into the domain dominated by the terrible goddess, Shiok, and her spawn. It is known as the Feral Wilds, and it is a place of horrific savagery and predation. Wild, ferocious animals rip one another to pieces and bones and carcasses litter the forest floor. Truly, it is a battleground - of creatures living in fear, starvation, disease - where the night brings terror, and each day is brutal and uncertain. Tales tell of these wild lands as also being the home of the horrifying Ferals - Greenmen who have devolved into savage beasts, followers of Shiok's vicious design, who hunt other living creatures and eat them alive, dining on blood and bone. The terrifying feral forests of Shiok are filled with the shrieks of ferocious beasts. Heskan culture is largely unknown. Certain scholars state there are hidden villages within the roots of trees and amongst the high-leafed canopy. Heskans are said to have earth-shaded skin, from dark brown to light olive and sage, with hair of matching color. Their limbs are long and are generally of tall and lithe stature. Besides the Ferals who feed on flesh, it is assumed the Heskan subsist on nutritious berries, seeds, and other plants. Greenmen, druidic adherents of green magics, are sylvan naturalists who revere the forests as sacred - often they will protect them with their lives against those who encroach upon the wooded grounds. Historically, Greenmen have also been present outside of Hesk, as in the monumental Fall of Isindyl, a once-magnificent Arch-tree far south of Shasir, when vengeant Greenmen warred against the incursion of the Demonic Kings.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Timeline: What are the Eras?","href":"codex/timeline.html#what-are-the-eras","kind":"codex","text":"There are six major eras of time from the beginning of creation to the present day. 0 The Primordial Era, or prehistory, is era zero. Here they are listed:","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Timeline: The Primordial Era","href":"codex/timeline.html#the-primordial-era","kind":"codex","text":"0-~5000000 to 0-0 An era of prehistory and pre-civilization. Humanoids lived as gatherers and clansfolk, foraging and living in the wilds. The majority of era zero was ruled by the Arch Dragons, whose might and totality of reign would never be rivaled again. The Skylords - the great avians - defeated the Arch Dragons with the aid of Hesk, and imprisoned Salaseth, the Dragon God, within Nargarond, in an act known as The Sealing. In the wake of the dragons' defeat, humanity was able to progress. The era ended with the construction of humanity's first civilization, Ultravis, built by the aghorians.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Timeline: The First Era","href":"codex/timeline.html#the-first-era","kind":"codex","text":"I-0 to I-3088 Generally known as the Age of Conquest. A long era that began with conquest and exploration, and carried the longest time span of uninterrupted peace. Though many wars were fought over territories and regions, many refer to the First Era as the \"Golden Age\". Out of Ultravis, the aghorians explored and conquered, and over the centuries became the first people who would spawn the majority of cultures and traditions across the world. In time, they would be completely divorced from the lands of their origin. Notably, the Vingian Empire would be recognized as the first empire in history, constructing their reach of power from their legendary city, Seltandor the Great. The prosperity of the First Era would end when the aghorian sorcerers of Ultravis would cast Infinity to prevent t","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Timeline: The Second Era","href":"codex/timeline.html#the-second-era","kind":"codex","text":"II-0 to II-1500 Generally known as the Dark Age. An era of darkness and ruin, marred by a horrible curse. In the wake of Ril'gorah, the kingdoms and cities across the world would be brought to smoldering rubble - fire would rain from the sky and the lands were thrown into great upheaval. Ril'gorah, an aghorian phrase meaning \"The Great Dying\", would bring darkness and pain for a thousand years. In or around ~II-50, the kings of yore, those whose kingdoms were reduced to rubble and their people ravaged by famine, raised their vengeful banners against mysterious figures who arrived during the cataclysm. The Val'norain, aghorian for \"Those From the Beyond\", were ascendant beings who held great power and were blamed by the Kings' for the havoc and destruction. The Kings' War would rage for cen","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Timeline: The Third Era","href":"codex/timeline.html#the-third-era","kind":"codex","text":"III-0 to III-1354 Generally known as the Dawn Age. An era of reconstruction following the suffering of the Dark Age. Aghor was elevated to heights of global worship as the one ancient God of all, who led the lands out of utter desolation. Villages, cities, and new empires were built atop the ashes of darkness, the new light of the shining suns giving hope to the wanting people. The Concord of the Second Era would amicably dissolve as its members faded into the legends of time, lauded with the highest reverence for their unimaginable achievements. The kingdoms in the centuries to follow would grow from the seeds they planted. New borders were drawn and great wars were waged by new people for resources in the new world. The cycles of war would not end, though there would be pockets of peace ","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Timeline: The Fourth Era","href":"codex/timeline.html#the-fourth-era","kind":"codex","text":"IV-0 to IV-2320 Generally known as the Age of Divinity. An age of new gods, great power, and wars unlike the world had yet seen. The Empire of Raim would bring their dominance across the sea, establishing states of rule within Syr Sable, and Essen Revir. Following the act of the Godform, drowned in limitless mana, as the Infinite was roused to become unbound. It was discovered that the potential of the soul had been fundamentally altered. Divine ascension of souls became possible, and over the centuries, new gods arose to form new pantheons in the absence of Aghor. Because of this, the lands of the Raim Empire would crumble in time, as the newly ascended Gods and their fervent followers would rise. God-Emperor Elraim, in his fury, would battle the rebel gods, ending wars and the dreams of ","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Timeline: The Fifth Era","href":"codex/timeline.html#the-fifth-era","kind":"codex","text":"V-0 to Present [V-44] Following Elraim's death, the loss of so many great lords and political dignitaries created a chaotic political landscape. To the world's dismay, the mythic Kingdom of Sorcery was obliterated. From across Ilium Aghor, to the lands of Syr Sable and beyond, nations held memorials to their fallen leaders and ceremonies for new coronations. In Ilium Aghor, the Falan Empire remains a superpower, pushing through the desert to join borders with their sister capital of Myrna. In Syr Sable, the royal queendom of Yollonde bolsters against the Red Scourge. The houses within Elen Asva quarrel over lordship. And of Far Nybei, a tyrant seems to lead Kaiden into a grim future. In a new age of promise, it is unknown what is to come.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Timeline: Eras","href":"codex/timeline.html#eras","kind":"codex","text":"Six eras, labeled 0 through V. From the accounts above and the Calendar . 0 The Primordial Era 0-~5000000 to 0-0 — prehistory; ended with Ultravis. I The First Era I-0 to I-3088 — Age of Conquest; ended with Ril'gorah. II The Second Era II-0 to II-1500 — Dark Age; ended at the Battle of Dawn. III The Third Era III-0 to III-1354 — Dawn Age; ended with the Godform . IV The Fourth Era IV-0 to IV-2320 — Age of Divinity. V The Fifth Era V-0 to Present [V-44] — present age.","image":"","private":false,"owner":""},{"title":"Timeline: Dates","href":"codex/timeline.html#dates","kind":"codex","text":"Known years drawn from the Calendar, Lore, and articles. II-50 Kings' War begins (around this year). Second Era II-1440 The Dark begins its cleansing. Second Era III-1213 Long Battle of Nilheen. Third Era III-1231 Aghoras joins the Empire of Raim. Third Era III-1249 Ilium Aghor under the Empire. Third Era IV-0 Fourth Era begins. Fourth Era IV-470 Galand Helviath born; meets Vaerek Rathkin . Invasion of the K'bas. IV-1621 Rise of the Eren Dominion. Fourth Era · Ilium Legendarium IV-2150 Horlg the Vile chains Angham. History of the Ages: Volume III IV-2320 Fourth Era ends. Fourth Era V-0 Fifth Era begins. Fifth Era V-9 Vaerek Rathkin born. V-43 Year of the Pink Sapling. Calendar V-44 Year of Threads — present. Calendar","image":"","private":false,"owner":""}];
